\chapter*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}
\markboth{Abstract}{}

Short-form video platforms such as TikTok structure user attention as a rapid sequence of binary micro-decisions: continue watching, or swipe away. Billions of such decisions are made every day, shaping what a population sees, knows, and acts on, though the data behind them rarely leaves the companies that collect it. Platform metrics and much of the surrounding public discourse treat continuous, uninterrupted viewing time as a proxy for user engagement, yet recent behavioural evidence suggests that prolonged, uninterrupted scrolling can reflect passive, dissociative consumption as well as goal-directed attention. This thesis investigates whether electroencephalography (EEG), recorded unobtrusively during naturalistic short-form video browsing, can discriminate the neural state preceding a voluntary skip decision from the neural state during sustained, non-skip viewing, and whether any such discrimination exceeds what is achieved by the conventional Engagement Index ($\beta/(\alpha+\theta)$), a composite band-power ratio drawn from human-factors neuroscience.

Twenty-five participants ($n=25$; 13 female, 12 male; age $M=24.6$, $SD=3.0$) were recorded with a consumer-grade, four-channel Muse S headband (AF7, AF8, TP9, TP10) while freely browsing a TikTok ``For You'' feed, reset before each participant, for 25 minutes in a private, distraction-free setting, following a two-minute resting baseline. Manual swipe events were synchronized to the EEG stream via keypress markers. A literature-motivated labeling scheme operationalized two classes: \texttt{SKIP}, drawn from a 3.0-second SKIP period ending 2.0 seconds before each swipe (a pre-swipe gap exceeding both the typical reported onset of the readiness potential (Bereitschaftspotential) and directly measured pre-movement electromyographic (EMG) onset in a self-paced reaching task), and \texttt{STAY}, comprising all remaining continuous, non-boundary viewing. From this labeling, 236 univariate features spanning seven frequency bands, four electrodes, and the raw broadband signal were engineered, including statistics of band strength, Hjorth parameters, and two features defined in the present thesis that count peaks of band strength per second (Peak Frequency, Macro Frequency).

Class separability, quantified via Cohen's $d$, was higher for every frequency band, every electrode and most statistical descriptors than for the Engagement Index; the single strongest feature, raw-signal standard deviation at TP10, exceeded the Engagement Index's separability by about ninefold. In logistic regression benchmarking against a coin-flip baseline, the Engagement Index model scored near chance within participants and was one-sided across participants: it labeled about 90\% of all windows \texttt{STAY}, which raised \texttt{STAY} recall without separating \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP}. Three candidate features identified through the Cohen's $d$ ranking, namely TP10 raw standard deviation, AF7 high-gamma mean and AF8 delta peak frequency, each yielded statistically significant improvements over the Engagement Index in \texttt{SKIP} detection at the inter-subject level, and, in \texttt{SKIP} precision only, for AF7 high-gamma mean and AF8 delta peak frequency at the intra-subject level, though absolute performance remained close to chance and, in the inter-subject setting, stayed below the chance level of about 50\%. A secondary behavioural analysis further showed that swipes cluster into rapid, individually variable ``burst'' sequences rather than occurring as uniformly spaced discrete events.

These findings indicate that the conventional Engagement Index captures only a limited, asymmetric component of the neural signal associated with short-form video browsing, and that more granular, region- and frequency-specific EEG features carry additional, currently underexploited discriminative information about imminent disengagement. The thesis further contributes a fully reproducible, open-source recording and analysis pipeline for consumer-grade EEG in naturalistic digital-media settings, and discusses the neuroscientific, psychological, behavioural, and public-health implications of these results, while explicitly flagging EMG and powerline-noise contamination as unresolved confounds requiring artifact-controlled replication.

\chapter{Introduction}
\label{chap:introduction}

\section{Motivation}

On TikTok, each swipe ends a micro-decision: continue watching, or swipe away. TikTok also carries health messages \cite{mccashin_using_2023}. A viewer swipes health messages away like any other video.

Social media designers are tasked with maximising time on site \cite{baughan_i_2022}. Yet long viewing can reflect absorbed attention or zoned-out scrolling \cite{baughan_i_2022, knierim_flow_2026} (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_short_form}).

The present thesis therefore studies the seconds before a swipe. Can electroencephalography (EEG) tell the seconds before a swipe apart from sustained viewing? And how well does the Engagement Index (EI) \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}, the standard EEG measure of engagement, separate the same seconds from sustained viewing? The EI divides beta power by the sum of alpha and theta power, \(\beta/(\alpha+\theta)\).

In one functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) study, brain activity predicted how much of each video viewers watched before skipping \cite{tong_brain_2020}. The viewers lay in a scanner, saw the same 32 videos and skipped with a button. A consumer EEG headband lets a viewer browse a TikTok feed on a phone. EEG gives less spatial detail and little access to deep brain regions. On the other hand, a small consumer EEG headband like the one used in the present study may prove less obstrusive than fMRI.

\section{Defining Engagement}
\label{sec:intro_engagement_definition}
\label{sec:working_definition}

Different research fields define engagement differently (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definitions}). The present thesis therefore states a working definition before measuring anything.

\begin{center}
\fbox{%
\begin{minipage}{0.92\textwidth}
\vspace{0.5em}
\textbf{Working definition (this thesis).} \textit{Engagement is a viewer's attention to the content on screen, held against the always-available next video, and ending at the latest at the swipe.}
\vspace{0.5em}
\end{minipage}}
\end{center}

The word \emph{attention} leaves out dissociated viewing, in which users use a feed without really paying attention to what they are doing \cite{baughan_i_2022}. The next video is always one thumb movement away. Each swipe has a timestamp. The swipe therefore dates the latest end of engagement. Engagement may end earlier without a visible sign.

The working definition holds only where a screen and a swipe exist. Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated} proposes a general definition for other settings. Watch time, sustained viewing and the \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} labels (Section~\ref{sec:op_stay_skip}) are indicators, meaning measurable signs of engagement. In the present thesis it is assumed, that no single indicator is engagement itself. Indicators may only hint at the possible presence of engagement.

\section{Engagement in Health Communication}
\label{sec:intro_health_communication}

Engagement is a precondition for the effect of a health intervention \cite{yardley_understanding_2016} (Section~\ref{sec:health_communication_literature}). Theories of how a health story persuades start from an audience that stays with the story \cite{green_role_2000, moyer-guse_toward_2008}. The present thesis looks at the step before persuasion: the seconds before a viewer swipes away.

\section{Research Gap}
\label{sec:intro_research_gap}

To the author's knowledge, four gaps lead to the research questions. Section~\ref{sec:lit_gaps} lists all gaps in detail.

\textbf{Gap 1: Time spent is an unvalidated indicator.} Health and social media research measures engagement mostly by amount of use, such as time spent and likes \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017, trunfio_conceptualising_2021}. Yet ``more engagement does not necessarily equate to more effective engagement'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. To help fill exactly that gap, the present thesis studies the swipe-event.

\textbf{Gap 2: Health communication research seldom uses EEG.} Perski et al.\ and Yardley et al.\ list body signals used to measure engagement, and name no EEG \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017, yardley_understanding_2016}. Although this study is not limited to health communication but instead examines the general principles of short‑form video communication termination (the swipe), it records EEG to help close this gap.

\textbf{Gap 3: The EI comes from a different task.} Pope et al.\ chose the EI for a computer task modelled on flight-deck work \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}. To the author's knowledge, no study reports the EI before a self-paced action comparable to the short-form video swipe. The present thesis benchmarks the EI in this setting.

\textbf{Gap 4: Engagement lacks a general definition.} O'Brien and Toms found no agreed definition of engagement \cite{obrien_what_2008}. Yardley et al.\ call for ``defining engagement more consistently and appropriately'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. The working definition holds only where a screen and a swipe exist. The present thesis proposes a general definition as a starting point for other fields.

Gaps 1 to 3 lead to research questions 1 and 2. Gap 4 leads to research question 3 (Section~\ref{sec:intro_research_questions}).

\section{Research Questions}
\label{sec:intro_research_questions}

The present thesis asks three research questions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Separability:} Can EEG from a consumer headband with four electrodes tell the seconds before a swipe (\texttt{SKIP}) apart from sustained viewing (\texttt{STAY}), while viewers browse TikTok freely?
    \item \textbf{Benchmark comparison:} How well do single EEG features separate \texttt{SKIP} from \texttt{STAY}, compared with the EI? Two measures answer the question. Cohen's $d$ measures how far the \texttt{SKIP} average and the \texttt{STAY} average lie apart, free of any unit. Logistic regression models predict \texttt{SKIP} or \texttt{STAY} for each second of recording, within each participant and on new participants.
    \item \textbf{Definition:} Which general definition of engagement, bound to no platform and no measuring instrument, do the reviewed definitions and the results of the present thesis suggest as a starting point for continuous research in potential other fields investigating engagement?
\end{enumerate}

Answering the three questions required a recording and analysis pipeline built for self-paced swipes. The pipeline is public and forms one contribution of the present thesis (Section~\ref{sec:contributions}).

\section{Approach}

Twenty-five participants ($n=25$) each wore a consumer Muse~S headband with four electrodes. After a two-minute resting baseline, each participant browsed a TikTok feed alone for 25 minutes. At every swipe, the participant pressed a key on a laptop. The keypress marked the swipe in the EEG (Sections~\ref{sec:method_participants}--\ref{sec:method_experimental_protocol}).

Four labeling parameters set the \texttt{SKIP} windows and the \texttt{STAY} windows around each keypress (Section~\ref{sec:method_skipstay_labeling_strategy}). A sensitivity analysis varied three of the four labeling parameters (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}). Each 1.0~s window gave 236 features (Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_engineering}). Cohen's $d$ ranked the 236 features and the EI. Logistic regression then tested the EI against a coin-flip baseline, and three features picked from the ranking against the EI (Section~\ref{sec:method_result_synthesis_pipeline}). Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated} answers the third research question.

\section{Contributions}
\label{sec:contributions}

Chapter~\ref{chap:results} reports six contributions of the present thesis, one section each:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{The experiment} (Section~\ref{sec:results_exp_learnings}). Twenty-five participants browsed TikTok freely. A consumer headband recorded the EEG, and a keypress marked every swipe.
    \item \textbf{The reusable pipeline} (Section~\ref{sec:results_pipeline}). The full pipeline is all code from recording to results. The code is public for reuse.
    \item \textbf{Swipe bursts} (Section~\ref{sec:results_burst_analysis}). Swipes often come in quick runs, and participants differ widely. The bursts are a secondary result.
    \item \textbf{Engagement Index performance} (Section~\ref{sec:results_engagement_index}). Every frequency band, every electrode and the raw signal separated \texttt{SKIP} from \texttt{STAY} better than the EI. No model predicted \texttt{SKIP} or \texttt{STAY} clearly better than chance.
    \item \textbf{Discussion} (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}). The Discussion asks what the results may mean. Muscle activity and powerline-noise, the 50~Hz electrical noise of power lines, may partly explain the EEG differences.
    \item \textbf{A proposed definition of engagement} (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated}). A general definition names no platform and no measuring instrument. Four reporting requirements and an untested hypothesis for cheaper field measures follow.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Thesis Structure}

Chapter~\ref{chap:literature} reviews related studies and ends with seven gaps. Chapter~\ref{chap:methods} describes the experiment, the \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} labels, the full pipeline and the statistical tests. Chapter~\ref{chap:results} reports the six contributions of Section~\ref{sec:contributions}. Chapter~\ref{chap:conclusion} answers the three research questions, lists the limitations and names future research. Chapter~\ref{chap:plain_language} answers common questions in plain language. Appendix~\ref{app:german_summary} gives a German summary.


