\section{Adjacent Study}
\label{sec:adjacent_studies}
% CITE-NEEDED P02: "One published study sits closest to the present thesis" | was: precedent | adjacent \cite{rahman_detection_2025} | quote verified against source PDF
One published study sits closest to the present thesis: Rahman et al.\ record EEG while people watch short-form video, and train a classifier to tell short-form video viewing apart from other activities \cite{rahman_detection_2025}. Rahman et al.\ present the classifier as a detector of Short-form Video Addiction, although the paper reports no measure of addiction, such as an addiction questionnaire. The present thesis calls the study of Rahman et al.\ the \emph{adjacent study}. The adjacent study records EEG with a consumer-grade Muse S headband (the paper names both the first and the second generation, Gen~1 and Gen~2) at four scalp electrodes, TP9, AF7, AF8 and TP10, sampled at 256~Hz, plus two auxiliary channels of the headband, which the paper does not describe further; the band-wise analysis runs on all six channels. The electrode labels follow the international 10--20 system, a standard map of positions on the scalp. In a laboratory, a small sample of student volunteers ($n=10$, ages 20--35) watches short-form video on their own phones, in their own feeds, TikTok included, and may swipe on at any moment. The present thesis calls self-paced viewing of an algorithmic feed, with a swipe possible at any moment, \textit{naturalistic use}, and the feed itself the \emph{infinite feed}.

The adjacent study labels whole blocks of time. A 15-minute block of short-form video viewing is the positive class; a block of other daily activity without media consumption on a smartphone is the negative class, balanced 1:1. The classifier of the adjacent study therefore answers one question: is this person inside the infinite feed, or not? Swipes play no role in the viewing-versus-not-viewing label. The two blocks may also differ in posture, gaze and hand movement: a person watching short-form video looks down at a phone and moves a thumb, while a person doing something else may not. Part of the reported accuracy may therefore come from body movement as well as from brain activity.

The adjacent study pairs EEG with electrodermal activity, a measure of skin conductance, from an Empatica E4 wristband. Neural networks learn one shared representation of the EEG and the skin-conductance signal. The purpose of the shared representation is a sensor swap: learn with the headband, then use the less obtrusive wristband alone. Evaluation repeatedly trains on 80\% of the data and tests on the remaining 20\% ($k$-fold cross-validation). The paper does not state whether windows from one 15-minute block can land in both the training data and the test data. If windows from one block land in both, the classifier can partly recognise the block itself, much like a student who has seen the exam questions during practice, and the reported accuracy may overstate performance on a new block. The adjacent study also reports no test on participants left out of training. Averaged over ten runs, the classifier reaches 76.3\% accuracy from EEG alone and 84.2\% from EEG and wristband together. For the sensor swap, the wristband alone reaches 71.5\%, and 80.2\% after training with help from the EEG.

On frequency, the adjacent study applies a band-stop filter at 50~Hz and the multiples of 50~Hz, and the paper names a ``110V 50Hz'' power supply. The ethics approval of the adjacent study comes from the University of Massachusetts Lowell, in the United States, where power lines carry alternating current at 60~Hz. The adjacent study then splits the signal into five bands: Delta 1--4, Theta 4--8, Alpha 7.5--13, Beta 13--30, and Gamma 30--44~Hz. The analysed spectrum stops at 44~Hz, so the mismatch between the 50~Hz filter and the 60~Hz power lines likely leaves the reported band results largely unaffected. Beta and Gamma are reported as the most informative bands, each above 80\% accuracy.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} The adjacent study labels whether a person is inside the infinite feed; the present thesis on the other hand labels two states inside the infinite feed, anchored to each self-paced swipe: \texttt{STAY}, sustained viewing without a swipe, and \texttt{SKIP}, the seconds just before the swipe. Because the labels differ, the accuracies of the present thesis and of the adjacent study measure different things and are not directly comparable. More specifically, the present thesis asks whether EEG features with a known meaning, such as the strength of one frequency band, separate \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP} better than the EI does (Chapter~\ref{chap:results}). The adjacent study analyses the spectrum up to 44~Hz, whereas the present thesis analyses the spectrum up to 100~Hz, including the high-gamma (40--60~Hz) and very-high-gamma (60--100~Hz) bands.

\section{Secondary Adjacent Studies}
\label{sec:secondary_adjacent_studies}
Six further studies record EEG in connection with short-form video, all with laboratory EEG systems of 32 to 64 electrodes.

\textbf{Short-video recommendation (Zhang et al.; He et al.)} \cite{zhang_eeg-svrec_2024, he_understanding_2023}. \emph{Research goal:} use EEG to improve short-video recommendation (Zhang et al.) and to detect how immersed a viewer feels (He et al.). \emph{Design:} two papers from one research group report the same lab study with 30 participants. Each participant gets a fresh account on a real platform, trained by ten hours of browsing; the researchers then compose 15-minute sessions of 20--30 personalised, non-personalised and random videos. Participants swipe freely while a 64-channel cap records at 1,000~Hz, filtered to 0.5--50~Hz. After each session, participants re-watch the first seconds of each video and rate the video from 1 to 5 on several scales; Zhang et al.\ call six of the scales, among them valence, interest and immersion, \emph{affective engagement}, and He et al.\ predict the immersion rating. \emph{Result:} adding EEG raises the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) of the recommendation models, the chance that a model scores a randomly picked positive case above a randomly picked negative case (0.5 = guessing, 1 = perfect), by at most about 0.04, and in a few models lowers the AUC slightly. He et al.\ predict high versus low immersion with 69.1\% accuracy without EEG and 70.1\% with EEG. \emph{Limits:} the ratings come from memory after the session, and Zhang et al.\ and He et al.\ do not report keeping each participant on one side of the training/test split, so viewings of one participant may land on both sides, the leak described for the adjacent study (Section~\ref{sec:adjacent_studies}).

\textbf{Opinion polarisation (Du et al.)} \cite{du_understanding_2025, du_eeg_2025}. \emph{Research goal:} show whether one-sided short videos shift a viewer's opinion of a historical or fictional figure, and whether EEG reveals the shift. \emph{Design:} two papers report one dataset of 23 participants. The design has three steps. In a first lab session with EEG, a web page built by the researchers plays, for each of ten such figures, one one-minute clip praising the figure and one clip criticising the figure, mixed with film-clip distractors; a ``Next One'' button skips a clip after 30~seconds. Over the following six days, participants watch clips on the same web page daily without EEG, and each figure appears to a participant only in praising clips or only in criticising clips, decided at random per figure. A second lab session repeats the first. A classifier then guesses, for each participant--figure pair, whether the six days praised or criticised the figure. Input: alpha-band power at one forehead electrode, Fpz, from a 64-channel cap filtered to 0.5--50~Hz, averaged over the praising and the criticising clip of the figure in both lab sessions. \emph{Result:} opinion ratings of a figure rise after six praising days and fall after six criticising days; the classifier reaches 64.1\% accuracy and an AUC of 0.672. \emph{Limits:} the five cross-validation folds are drawn at random over participant--figure pairs, so pairs of one participant can land in training and test data; Fpz was chosen because Fpz scored best in the same cross-validation, which likely makes the 64.1\% optimistic; and the six browsing days carry no EEG.

\textbf{Short-video advertising (Zhang and Zhang)} \cite{zhang_how_2025-1}. \emph{Research goal:} find which selling approach (transactional or relational) and which displaying style (high or low enthusiasm and humour) of an online celebrity evokes more ``neural engagement''. \emph{Design:} 25 participants watch the same 64 TikTok advertisements of 10--35~s each while a 64-channel cap records at 250~Hz. Neural engagement here means four EEG band-power ratios: emotion, relaxation, attention and cognitive load. The relaxation index is the EI, $\beta/(\alpha+\theta)$, turned into the negative logarithm, so a higher relaxation score means a lower EI. \emph{Result:} transactional selling and the high displaying style evoke higher values on most of the four indices (analysis of variance, ANOVA). \emph{Limits:} each advertisement plays to the end, so viewing is not self-paced, and the study compares group averages without predicting the state of a single viewer.

\textbf{Remaining studies.} Wade and Dumitru are unpublished student theses. \emph{Wade asks:} does TikTok addiction impair response inhibition? EEG runs during a fixed 3-minute TikTok compilation and a following Go/NoGo task, in which a participant presses a button for one signal and holds back for another \cite{wade_investigating_2024}. \emph{Dumitru asks:} does TikTok-style content impair sustained attention? Participants scroll for 5~minutes through the own TikTok or Instagram feed; the EEG of the 5~minutes enters one left-versus-right alpha comparison, and a $\beta/\alpha$ engagement index is computed during attention tasks at baseline and after viewing \cite{dumitru_does_2025}. \emph{Yan et al.\ ask:} does a tendency toward short-video addiction go with weaker attention control? Yan et al.\ correlate an addiction questionnaire score with EEG recorded during an attention task, and record no viewing \cite{yan_mobile_2024}.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} In the six studies, no label or comparison is anchored to the moment of a swipe: Zhang et al.\ and He et al.\ log every swipe but leave the moment of the swipe unused, keeping only the share of each video watched, Du et al.\ allow a skip only after 30~seconds, Dumitru does not record the swipes, and the remaining studies involve no swipe. The present thesis anchors every \texttt{SKIP} label to one time-stamped, self-paced swipe in the TikTok ``For You'' infinite feed, and analyses the spectrum above 50~Hz, which the 0.5--50~Hz filters of Zhang et al., He et al.\ and Du et al.\ remove. The present thesis also records with a consumer headband, which, unlike the laboratory systems of the six studies, is affordable for a single consumer and can be worn during naturalistic use outside a laboratory.


\section{Recording Software}
\label{sec:libraries}
One route from a Muse headband to a laptop is \texttt{muselsl}, an open-source tool created by Barachant et al.\ \cite{barachant_muselsl_2026}. \texttt{muselsl} connects to the Muse EEG headband over Bluetooth, and the command \texttt{muselsl stream} passes the EEG on to the laptop in near real time; further \texttt{muselsl} commands display and record the EEG. \texttt{muselsl} runs as a command-line tool and as an importable Python library, and the current documentation lists the Muse S used in the present thesis as supported.

\texttt{muselsl stream} sends the EEG into the Lab Streaming Layer (LSL), a system built to unify the collection of time-series data in research experiments. LSL puts each data source on the network as a separate stream, stamps every sample with a time, keeps the streams synchronized in time, and gives near-real-time access to every stream; the \texttt{muselsl} documentation calls LSL standard in EEG-based brain-computer interface research. As far as the \texttt{muselsl} documentation shows, \texttt{muselsl} offers no way to mark a keypress in the EEG recording and no automatic reconnection after a Bluetooth dropout.

A second route is Mind Monitor, an app that records the Muse on a phone; the adjacent study used Mind Monitor on Android \cite{rahman_detection_2025}. The adjacent study labels whole 15-minute blocks (Section~\ref{sec:adjacent_studies}), so the adjacent study needs no marker for a single event such as a swipe.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} Every \texttt{SKIP} label of the present thesis is anchored to one swipe, marked by a keypress on the laptop, and every \texttt{STAY} label covers the remaining viewing time minus a pre-swipe gap and a post-swipe gap around each keypress (Sections~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition} and \ref{sec:op_labeling_params}), so the EEG recording needs a time mark for every keypress and a record of every Bluetooth dropout. A recording pipeline by the author of the present thesis therefore starts \texttt{muselsl stream}, stamps each keypress with the laptop clock, converts the keypress time into LSL time, flags the EEG sample that falls within a defined tolerance window of the keypress, and restarts the stream in a new file after a Bluetooth dropout (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_recording_pipeline}). Preprocessing then measures each Bluetooth dropout from the timestamps between consecutive files, and no analysis window crosses a Bluetooth dropout (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_preprocessing_pipeline}).

\runninghead{Existing Definitions of Engagement}
\section{Defining Engagement: Existing Definitions and Their Gaps}
\label{sec:engagement_definitions}

The fields that measure engagement do not define engagement consistently. O'Brien and Toms found no agreed definition when O'Brien and Toms set out to deconstruct the term for human--computer interaction (HCI) \cite{obrien_what_2008}. More than a decade later, a systematic review of social media engagement describes engagement as a term with many meanings and many dimensions \cite{trunfio_conceptualising_2021}. In digital behaviour-change research, Yardley et al.\ call for ``defining engagement more consistently and appropriately'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. Five fields bear on the present thesis.

\textbf{Human--computer interaction.} O'Brien and Toms interviewed 17 users of web search, online shopping, webcasting, and gaming applications. O'Brien and Toms define engagement as ``a quality of user experience characterized by attributes of challenge, positive affect, endurability, aesthetic and sensory appeal, attention, feedback, variety/novelty, interactivity, and perceived user control''. In the model of O'Brien and Toms, engagement unfolds as a four-stage process: point of engagement, sustained engagement, disengagement, and re-engagement \cite{obrien_what_2008}. A recent review of EEG-based engagement research adopts the attribute list of O'Brien and Toms \cite{bellos_methods_2025}. The four stages rest on the interviews, in which participants recall a past experience, and O'Brien and Toms note that a recalled experience is a ``selected interpretation'' of the event \cite{obrien_what_2008}.

\textbf{Marketing and platform analytics.} Trunfio and Rossi, following Hollebeek, describe customer engagement as the positive, brand-related cognitive, emotional, and behavioural activity of a consumer during interactions with a brand. Trunfio and Rossi review social media engagement and find the behavioural dimension to be the most common way to measure engagement: counts of likes, comments, shares, and views, and indices derived from the counts \cite{trunfio_conceptualising_2021}. Salminen et al.\ measure engagement with short-video advertising as viewer churn, the share of viewers who leave during playback, in 12 TikTok advertisements of one company. TikTok reports churn per quarter of a video, counted over all viewers of an advertisement. Most viewers left within the first quarter of an advertisement \cite{salminen_engagement_2024}. Churn shows in which quarter of a video viewers leave, while the state of each viewer before leaving stays unmeasured.

\textbf{Human-factors neuroscience and later uses of the EI.} Pope et al.\ tested candidate EEG indices with six participants in a computer task modelled on flight-deck work. A feedback loop switched a tracking task between the participant and the computer whenever an index rose or fell over a time interval. Among the candidate indices, the ratio of beta power to the sum of alpha and theta power, \(\beta/(\alpha+\theta)\), the EI, reflected task engagement best \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}. Pope et al.\ judged each index by the switching pattern of the loop, and report no separate measure of engagement. Nuamah et al., citing earlier work, define task engagement as ``effortful striving towards task goals''. Nuamah et al.\ used the EI as the input for recognising which of five mental tasks a participant performed, trained one classifier per participant, and report an average accuracy of 88.67\% across six participants, where guessing among five tasks gives 20\%. The recordings come from an existing dataset of 10-second trials, with 25 to 75 trials per participant \cite{nuamah_electroencephalography_2017}. In a controlled learning task, Apicella et al.\ classified two levels of cognitive engagement from the EI alone with 51 to 55\% accuracy, close to the 50\% that guessing between two levels gives, while features from twelve narrow frequency bands, combined across electrodes, reached about 77\%. Apicella et al.\ note that EEG signals change over time and differ between people, which makes classification across participants difficult \cite{apicella_eeg-based_2022}, and a recent review names the differences in EEG between people as a pressing issue in EEG engagement research \cite{bellos_methods_2025}. In a conference abstract, de Carvalho et al.\ treat the EI ratio as a vigilance index, that is, a marker of sustained attention. In the study of de Carvalho et al.\ with 15 students, three of four markers chosen for sustained attention, the EI ratio among them, separated a sustained-attention test from a cognitive-engagement test, while the markers chosen for engagement did not. De Carvalho et al.\ attribute the failure of one engagement marker, theta power at the front midline of the head, mainly to a resting baseline recorded after the effortful tests \cite{de_carvalho_selection_2026}.

\textbf{Digital health and behaviour-change intervention science.} Perski et al.\ define engagement with two components: the extent of use, such as the amount, frequency, duration, and depth of use, and a subjective experience characterised by attention, interest, and affect. Perski et al.\ describe engagement as ``a dynamic process'' that is expected to vary within and across individuals over time \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}. Yardley et al.\ describe engagement as a process that starts with a trigger and first use, followed by sustained engagement or disengagement, and separate moment-to-moment engagement with the intervention from engagement with the wider goals of the intervention. Yardley et al.\ note that an observed end of use can mean disengagement, or can mean that the user has learned enough to need the intervention no longer. Yardley et al.\ shift the target to \emph{effective engagement}, and note that ``more engagement does not necessarily equate to more effective engagement'' and that effective engagement ``may or may not require sustained engagement'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}.

\textbf{Learning analytics.} Karimah and Hasegawa review automatic engagement estimation and follow Fredricks et al., who divide engagement into behavioural, emotional, and cognitive engagement. Emotional engagement, measured from affective cues, is the type studied most often. The largest share of the reviewed studies (43\%) rates engagement by external observation, followed by self-report (20\%). Karimah and Hasegawa note that self-report carries bias, and that external observation limits the judgement of the actual effort of learners \cite{karimah_automatic_2022}.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} O'Brien and Toms reconstruct disengagement from recalled accounts, and an observed end of use, such as viewer churn counted per quarter of an advertisement or the end of use of a health intervention, shows roughly when people stopped but leaves the state of each person before stopping unmeasured. In small studies, the EI recognised five mental tasks well (Nuamah et al.), classified two engagement levels close to guessing (Apicella et al.), and behaved as a marker of sustained attention (de Carvalho et al.); classification across participants remains an open problem, and, to the author's knowledge, the behaviour of the EI in the seconds before a self-paced decision has not been reported. The present thesis states a working definition anchored to the swipe, an observed end of viewing (Section~\ref{sec:intro_engagement_definition}), tests whether the EI and other EEG features separate the seconds before each self-paced swipe from sustained viewing, within and across participants, and later proposes a general definition of engagement that names no platform and no measuring instrument (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated}).

\runninghead{Measuring Engagement in Health Communication}
\section{Measuring Engagement in the Health Communication Literature}
\label{sec:health_communication_literature}

The present section covers two bodies of work within health communication. The first body studies digital behaviour change interventions (DBCIs), such as health apps and health websites. The second body studies health messages on TikTok. Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definitions} gave the definitions of engagement from the DBCI work. The present section asks how the two bodies of work \emph{measure} engagement.

\paragraph{Three routes.} In the DBCI work, engagement with a health intervention is a precondition for the effectiveness of the health intervention \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. Perski et al.\ reviewed 117 articles on engagement with DBCIs and name three routes to measure engagement \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}. The present thesis calls the three routes the \emph{ask route}, the \emph{log route}, and the \emph{body route}.
\begin{itemize}
    \item The ask route: self-report questionnaires, interviews, or think-aloud studies.
    \item The log route: automated recording of use, such as logins and page views.
    \item The body route: recording of physiological or psychophysical correlates.
\end{itemize}
In the behavioural-science literature, the log route was the most common. Typical log-route measures were logins, time spent online, and the amount and type of content used. Body-route measures came from the computer-science and HCI literatures: cardiac activity, respiratory depth, electrodermal activity, and eye tracking. Perski et al.\ note two limits. Ask-route questionnaires typically measure engagement after a person has used the intervention, instead of during use. In the one study Perski et al.\ cite on the question, body-route measures and ask-route measures were only weakly associated \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}.

\paragraph{The log route in practice.} Yardley et al.\ judge that usage alone cannot be taken as a valid indicator of engagement. The evidence linking usage to health outcomes is mixed and largely correlational \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. Evans et al.\ review digital media for behaviour change and name inconsistent, non-standardised measures, such as engagement and reach, as the research gap cited most often. Evans et al.\ count objective dosage and exposure data from platform analytics among the strengths of social-media interventions. Evans et al.\ recommend dose--response designs, which test whether more exposure goes with more behaviour change, and state that ``what qualifies as a critical threshold for engagement in digital interventions has yet to be established'' \cite{evans_digital_2022}.

\paragraph{The body route in practice.} Yardley et al.\ list body-route measures of task engagement: skin conductance, heart rate, facial expression, and fMRI. According to Yardley et al., body-route measures can show which parts of an intervention attract attention or evoke emotional arousal in the short term, and add an objective record of user reactions to ask-route measures. Yardley et al.\ also rate body-route measures as often intrusive, expensive, and hard to scale. Body-route measures taken in a laboratory may lack ecological validity, meaning that laboratory results may not carry over to everyday use. Yardley et al.\ further warn that differences in attention ``may not always translate into differences in intervention use'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}.

\paragraph{Health messages on TikTok.} McCashin and Murphy systematically reviewed 24 studies of TikTok for public or mental health. In 20 of the 24 studies, the primary method was content analysis, which codes what the videos show \cite{mccashin_using_2023}. Three studies describe health messages on TikTok through content analysis and the counts TikTok displays, such as views, likes, and comments.

Basch et al.\ coded 100 videos under \#Coronavirus and all 17 posts of the World Health Organization (WHO) in April 2020. The 117 videos had 1{,}194{,}081{,}700 views in total. Fewer than 10\% of the videos mentioned transmission, symptoms, or prevention. WHO videos covered transmission and symptoms more often than consumer videos, yet still in fewer than 10\% of videos \cite{basch_covid-19_2022}. Very high reach therefore came with little health content. Basch et al.\ coded content and counts only, so the study does not show what viewers took in.

McCashin and Murphy also analysed the content of 29 Irish TikTok accounts: 2 public-health accounts, 5 charity accounts, and 22 personal creators. Both public-health videos came from one account, the Irish Health Service Executive. Public-health videos used humour in 0 of 2 videos and a popular sound in 0 of 2 videos. Personal creators used humour in 16 of 22 videos and a popular sound in at least 17 of 22 videos. Videos with a popular sound averaged 132{,}419 likes. Videos without a popular sound averaged 51{,}161 likes. McCashin and Murphy conclude that institutional accounts, meaning the public-health and charity accounts, used fewer TikTok features and received fewer likes, comments, and shares. The comparison uses no statistical test and rests on 2 public-health videos. McCashin and Murphy also ask whether TikTok analytics reflect in-depth user engagement that may lead to positive outcomes, and call for controlled studies that relate TikTok engagement to behaviour change \cite{mccashin_using_2023}.

Chen et al.\ analysed 355 COVID-19 videos from ``Healthy China'', the TikTok account of the National Health Commission of China, from January to April 2020. Chen et al.\ measure engagement as the counts of likes, shares, and comments. Regression models for count data linked longer video length to fewer likes (incidence rate ratio, $\text{IRR}=0.19$, $p<0.001$) and fewer comments ($\text{IRR}=0.39$, $p<0.001$). An IRR below 1 means fewer counts, and an IRR above 1 means more counts. The emotion of the title changed the link: longer videos with a positive title received more likes ($\text{IRR}=21.72$, $p=0.04$) and more comments ($\text{IRR}=10.14$, $p=0.047$) \cite{chen_factors_2021}. Chen et al.\ record the length of each video and do not record how long viewers watched.

\paragraph{The research agenda.} Zenone et al.\ wrote when TikTok had passed one billion monthly users, and videos tagged \#MentalHealth had 17 billion views. Zenone et al.\ propose six public-health research priorities for TikTok. Most of the six priorities concern the content on TikTok or TikTok as a company. One priority, on medical advice, also asks how users take up the health information received. Zenone et al.\ ask that research runs independently of TikTok, with conflicts of interest declared when TikTok funds or joins the research \cite{zenone_tiktok_2021}. For DBCI research, Yardley et al.\ list a related priority: to ``investigate and validate complementary and non-intrusive measures of effective engagement'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} The health communication literature reviewed here measures engagement mostly through the log route, as counts of use and the counts TikTok displays, and the body-route examples of Perski et al.\ and Yardley et al.\ do not include EEG. To the author's knowledge, no study in the health communication literature reviewed here records a body-route signal in the seconds before a viewer swipes a health message away, a moment that counts of use do not capture. The present thesis tests consumer EEG, recorded independently of TikTok and without data from TikTok itself (Section~\ref{sec:results_pipeline}), as one candidate for the complementary measures Yardley et al.\ call for, keeping in mind that a headband remains a body-route measure that Yardley et al.\ rate as often intrusive and hard to scale.

\section{Viewing Time as an Indicator of Engagement}
\label{sec:engagement_short_form}
Social media designers are tasked with maximising time on site \cite{baughan_i_2022}, and Knierim et al.\ advise platforms to measure the quality of the experience rather than time on the platform alone \cite{knierim_flow_2026}. To the author's knowledge, no study tests directly whether long viewing of short-form video means engagement. Four studies bear on the question from two sides: two \emph{daily-life studies} of social media use, and two \emph{lab EEG studies} of attention within a task. None of the four studies records EEG during short-form video viewing.

\textbf{Knierim et al.} \cite{knierim_flow_2026}. \emph{Research goal:} test whether social media produces flow, a state of deep and effortless absorption. 40 university students took part in a five-day field study. Tracking software logged the use of a preset list of phone apps. Three times a day, participants reconstructed the preceding hours and listed activities with flow. The survey did not ask about social media directly. Participants attributed 14 of 673 flow reports (2\%) to social media. Knierim et al.\ sort apps, not behaviours, into two categories. Passive apps are built around feeds and videos, for example Instagram, TikTok, and YouTube. Active apps serve direct communication. When a participant used more social media than usual in the hours before a report, the participant reported fewer flow episodes. The drop in flow was significant for passive apps and not significant for active apps. More use than usual also went with lower mental readiness, a mix of mood, sleepiness, and motivation, for both app categories. Knierim et al.\ suggest physiological signals such as EEG to tell genuine flow apart from absorption that only resembles flow. \emph{Limits:} the tracking software counts minutes per app, and participants recall flow hours later. No physiological signal is recorded.

\textbf{Baughan et al.} \cite{baughan_i_2022}. \emph{Research goal:} learn how feed design shapes normative dissociation. Normative dissociation is full absorption with reduced self-awareness and a reduced sense of control. Baughan et al.\ count both mindless scrolling and deep absorption in the content itself as normative dissociation. 43 U.S.\ participants used a custom Twitter app for four weeks, and the Twitter app changed its design features every week. One in-app question measured dissociation, ``I am currently using Chirp without really paying attention to what I am doing'', averaged per day. 11 participants were interviewed. Interviewees described getting ``lost'' in the feed and losing track of time. Custom lists and reading-history labels lowered dissociation. On days with more views of usage statistics, participants reported less dissociation. On days when participants closed the Twitter app through a time-limit prompt, participants reported more dissociation, and Baughan et al.\ read the time-limit prompt as a cue to notice the dissociation. Time spent reading, entered as a control variable, was not significantly associated with dissociation ($p=0.52$). \emph{Limits:} the feed shows text posts, dissociation is one self-reported item, and daily averages cannot show whether dissociation came before or after an action. No physiological signal is recorded.

\textbf{deBettencourt et al.} \cite{debettencourt_sustained_2021}. \emph{Research goal:} separate two kinds of attention in memory. \emph{Sustained attention} is attention held steady over time. \emph{Spatial attention} is attention aimed at one place on the screen. In three lab experiments, a cue first points to one place on the screen. Images of objects then appear. Later, participants mark where each object appeared, or which colour each object had. In the EEG experiment ($n=30$), a classifier reads the EEG after the cue and before the objects appear, and predicts whether an object will be remembered better or worse than the participant's median, at 57.2\% accuracy against a 50\% chance level. The classifier is trained and tested on averages over groups of trials, not on single trials. deBettencourt et al.\ read the 57.2\% prediction as sustained attention rising and falling from trial to trial. During trials with low sustained attention, the EEG still showed spatial attention at the cued place. deBettencourt et al.\ therefore conclude that low sustained attention is not the same as dropping out of the task. \emph{Limits:} each trial starts at a cue set by the experimenter, and the task offers no way to leave. The study does not test how sustained attention changes with time on task.

\textbf{De Carvalho et al.} \cite{de_carvalho_selection_2026}. \emph{Research goal:} find EEG frequency markers that separate sustained attention from cognitive engagement. After the two tests described in Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definitions}, the 15 students watched a 27-minute science lecture video while a 32-channel research cap recorded the EEG. During the lecture video, several markers changed along curves over time. The EI ratio, measured over central and parietal sites, rose and then fell. Theta power at the back of the head fell and then rose. Alpha power over parietal and occipital sites, a marker chosen for engagement, rose steadily. De Carvalho et al.\ conclude that a marker averaged over a whole task hides changes within the task. \emph{Limits:} the curves explain only about 1--2\% of the variation in the markers, and no marker correlated with test scores. The lecture video offers no way to skip.

\textbf{What the four studies show together.} In the two daily-life studies, time spent in a feed was not significantly associated with dissociation, and more passive use than usual went with fewer flow reports. In the two lab EEG studies, attention changed within a task on two different timescales: from trial to trial in deBettencourt et al., and along slow curves over 27 minutes in de Carvalho et al. In deBettencourt et al., a drop in sustained attention did not mean leaving the task.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} To the author's knowledge, no study has yet checked whether long viewing without a skip reflects engagement, and none of the four studies records a signal during short-form video viewing that could separate attention held on the content from continued viewing alone, a separation for which Knierim et al.\ suggest EEG. The present thesis records EEG during naturalistic use and puts all continuous viewing without a skip into one class, \texttt{STAY}, treated as a partial operationalisation of engagement rather than as engagement itself. The EEG analysis (Sections~\ref{sec:method_feature_engineering}--\ref{sec:method_result_synthesis_pipeline}) tests what separates \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP}, keeping in mind that, as in deBettencourt et al., \texttt{STAY} may contain stretches of low attention that do not end in a swipe.

\section{Parameter Choice in EEG Classification}
\label{sec:lit_parameter_choice}
An EEG classifier does not read a recording in one piece. The recording is first cut into windows. A window is a short slice of the recording. Each window becomes one example for the classifier. Two timing choices shape every window. The window length sets how long each window is. The window placement sets where each window sits relative to an event, such as a cue on the screen. Five studies show how window length and window placement change classification results. A separate line of work describes how window length and window placement are usually tested.

\textbf{Moontaha et al.} \cite{moontaha_online_2023}. \emph{Research goal:} classify emotions from wearable EEG in real time. In the main experiment, participants watched 16 short emotional videos, 8 with each of two consumer EEG devices. One of the two devices was the Muse~S. Moontaha et al.\ tested window lengths of 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5~s, on the recordings of the study and on a public dataset (AMIGOS). The 1-second window scored best for every emotion dimension, classifier, and device. In most cases, the score fell as the window grew longer. Moontaha et al.\ read the fall as a sign that the classifier needs more examples. A shorter window gives more examples per video. \emph{Limits:} the windows were shuffled before cross-validation, so neighbouring windows from the same video likely appear in both the training data and the test data, which may favour short windows.

\textbf{Tzimourta et al.} \cite{tzimourta_eeg_2019}. \emph{Research goal:} detect Alzheimer's disease from clinical EEG. 24 people took part: 14 patients and 10 healthy people of similar age. Participants sat relaxed with eyes closed. Window lengths ran from 5 to 12~s, in steps of 1~s. Accuracy rose at every step, in all six classification problems. The 12-second window scored best. \emph{Limits:} the target is a disease, and a disease stays the same across the whole recording.

\textbf{Window length: two directions.} Moontaha et al.\ found shorter windows better. Tzimourta et al.\ found longer windows better. The finding of Moontaha et al.\ covers 1--5~s. The finding of Tzimourta et al.\ covers 5--12~s. The targets differ as well. An emotion can change within one video. A disease stays the same across a recording, so a longer window may simply average more of the same state.

\textbf{Mi et al.} \cite{mi_exploring_2026}. \emph{Research goal:} improve motor-imagery classification by combining several window lengths. In motor imagery, a participant imagines moving a hand, a foot, or the tongue after a cue. On a public dataset of nine participants, Mi et al.\ tested single window lengths from 0.5 to 3~s. The best window length differed from participant to participant. For one participant, accuracy differed by about 13 percentage points between window lengths. A combination of several window lengths beat every single window length. \emph{Limits:} the window-length test used one dataset of nine participants.

\textbf{Rimbert et al.} \cite{rimbert_impact_2023}. \emph{Research goal:} test how the placement of a rest window changes motor-imagery results. 71 participants imagined left-hand or right-hand movements. A classifier separated imagined movement from rest. The rest window lasted 3~s and sat at one of four positions around the next cue, from 5~s before the cue to 1~s after the cue. The rest window furthest from the next cue gave the highest accuracy. The furthest rest window also sat closest to the end of the previous trial. The furthest rest window scored 4 percentage points above the rest window ending at the cue ($p<0.05$), and 8 points above the rest window overlapping the cue ($p<0.001$). Rimbert et al.\ warn that part of the gain may come from the previous trial. After a movement ends, real or imagined, beta power over the motor cortex rises above the resting level for a while. The rise peaks about 1.5~s after the movement. A rest window close to the previous trial can fall inside the rise. Measured against the raised level, the power drop during imagined movement looks larger than the true drop. Rimbert et al.\ therefore recommend at least 3~s between the end of one movement and the start of a rest window. The best position also differed between people. The furthest position was best for 52--55\% of participants, and the second-furthest position for 22--30\%. \emph{Limits:} every rest window lasted 3~s, and the study covers one motor-imagery task.

\textbf{How timing choices are tested.} A sensitivity analysis asks how much a result changes when a setting changes. The simplest design changes one setting and holds every other setting fixed, like turning one knob on a mixing desk while the other knobs stay put. The design is called one-at-a-time (OAT) \cite{saltelli_global_2008}. OAT never changes two settings together. OAT therefore cannot show an interaction, meaning a case where the effect of one setting depends on the value of another setting \cite{saltelli_global_2008}. OAT is described as the most popular sensitivity-analysis practice in published modelling work \cite{saltelli_how_2010}. Saltelli et al.\ argue that OAT explores the possible settings inefficiently, and recommend alternatives from statistical theory, such as experimental design and regression analysis \cite{saltelli_how_2010}. Moontaha et al., Tzimourta et al., and Mi et al.\ each vary window length one setting at a time. Rimbert et al.\ vary window placement one setting at a time. McCartney et al.\ vary two settings together.

\textbf{McCartney et al.} \cite{mccartney_optimal_2026}. \emph{Research goal:} find the best combination of time window and frequency band for motor-imagery classification. On a public dataset of 109 people, McCartney et al.\ built one model per person for every combination of 5 time windows and 23 frequency bands. The time windows differed in length and in start after the cue. A repeated-measures ANOVA, a standard test for comparing the same people across conditions, found an effect of time window ($p<0.001$) and an effect of frequency band ($p<0.001$). The interaction of time window and frequency band gave $p=0.044$, close to the usual 0.05 threshold, and McCartney et al.\ report the interaction as significant. The best combination for the whole group was 0--4~s after the cue at 4--12~Hz. The best time window differed between people: 0--2.25~s was best for 34 of the 109 participants, and 0--4~s for 31. \emph{Limits:} each per-person model learns from about 90 trials, and the best time window averages 57\% accuracy, close to the 50\% of guessing between the left and the right hand. The study is a preprint.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} Every study above either places its windows around an event set by the experimenter, such as a cue or a video, or cuts a resting recording with no event at all. EEG studies of self-paced movement do classify windows before the movement \cite{lew_detection_2012}, but, to the author's knowledge, no study varies window length and window placement before a self-paced action during media viewing, such as a swipe. The present thesis labels windows before each swipe as \texttt{SKIP} and varies three labeling parameters: the window length, the pre-swipe gap between the \texttt{SKIP} windows and the swipe, and the SKIP period, meaning the stretch that holds the \texttt{SKIP} windows. The pre-swipe gap and the SKIP period are crossed on a grid; the window length is varied one at a time (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}).

\section{Windows at Label Borders}
\label{sec:lit_label_borders}
A labeled EEG recording consists of stretches. Each stretch carries one label. In an emotion study, one stretch can be one video, labeled with the rating of the participant. In the present thesis, one stretch is either \texttt{STAY} or \texttt{SKIP}. A window that crosses the border of a stretch holds EEG from both sides of the border. Yet the window can carry only one label. One EEG study and one review handle windows at a border.

\textbf{Moontaha et al.} \cite{moontaha_online_2023}. Section~\ref{sec:lit_parameter_choice} introduced the emotion study of Moontaha et al. Moontaha et al.\ cut the EEG into windows without overlap. Every window inside a video gets the rating the participant gave that video. Where possible, Moontaha et al.\ align the windows with the end of the video. Otherwise, Moontaha et al.\ use only windows that lie completely inside the time range of the video, so windows that cross the start or the end of a video stay unused. \emph{Limits:} Moontaha et al.\ give no reason for leaving out windows that cross a video border, and report no test of windows at a border. The version read for the present thesis is the preprint.

\textbf{Frénay and Verleysen} \cite{frenay_classification_2014}. \emph{Research goal:} review what wrong labels do to a classifier, and how methods deal with wrong labels. Frénay and Verleysen name disagreement between experts as one source of wrong labels. For example, in electrocardiogram analysis, experts seldom agree on the exact borders of the waves in a heartbeat recording. One reviewed electrocardiogram study therefore removes the labels of examples close to a border between two classes, because experts label less reliably near such a border. The chance of a wrong label can differ between examples. An example that resembles the other class may get a wrong label more often. Wrong labels can lower the accuracy of a classifier and raise the number of training examples a classifier needs. Several reviewed works found that removing examples with wrong labels worked better than correcting the labels. Removing too few or too many examples can both lower performance. \emph{Limits:} the review treats wrong labels in general and includes no EEG study.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} In Moontaha et al., the experimenter sets every border, so the time of every border is known, and a window that crosses a border can be found and left out. In the present thesis, the viewer ends each video with a self-paced swipe, and the moment the decision to swipe begins is unknown, so even a window that lies completely inside one \texttt{STAY} or \texttt{SKIP} stretch may carry a wrong label. To the author's knowledge, no EEG study tests where to place a label border whose time is unknown; the present thesis moves the border between \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} by varying the pre-swipe gap and the SKIP period, and reports how the separation between \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} changes (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}).

\section{Class Separation Metric}
\label{sec:lit_class_separation}
A study that compares features measured in different units, such as microvolts and hertz, cannot compare the raw differences between two class averages directly. Cohen's \(d\) divides the difference between two class averages by the spread of the feature within the two classes, so the unit cancels out. One book defines Cohen's \(d\), and two EEG studies use Cohen's \(d\) to compare two groups of people.

\textbf{Cohen} \cite{cohen_statistical_1988}. \emph{Research goal:} give behavioural researchers tables that show how likely a study with a given number of participants is to detect an effect of a given size. Cohen defines \(d\) for two groups as the difference between the two group averages, divided by the standard deviation within the groups. Cohen calls \(d\) a ``pure'' number, free of the original unit, and argues that \(d\) helps especially where the raw unit is arbitrary, such as points on an attitude scale. For two groups that spread differently, Cohen divides by the square root of the average of the two variances. For two measurements taken from the same people, such as a sensory threshold measured on both sides of the body of each patient, Cohen gives a second version of \(d\), which first takes one difference per person and then divides the average of the differences by the standard deviation of the differences.

\textbf{Henao Isaza et al.} \cite{henao_isaza_comprehensive_2026}. \emph{Research goal:} develop a way to enlarge small EEG samples, tested on telling carriers of a gene mutation that causes Alzheimer's disease (PSEN1-E280A), who show no cognitive symptoms yet, apart from healthy non-carriers, using EEG recorded at rest. Henao Isaza et al.\ pool four EEG datasets and, aiming to balance age and sex, form samples with 2, 5, and 10 non-carriers per carrier. A decision tree selects the 100 most important features, and Cohen's \(d\) then describes how far carriers and non-carriers differ in each selected feature. \emph{Limits:} to reach the higher ratios, Henao Isaza et al.\ remove carriers, from 79 carriers at a ratio of 2 to 15 carriers at a ratio of 10, and the remaining carriers are older: the average age of the carriers rises from about 35 to 42 years, while the non-carriers stay near 30 years. Over the same steps, the largest \(d\) of an EEG feature rises from 1.22 to 2.28 in absolute value, and \(d\) for age rises from 1.10 to 2.78. The rise of \(d\) for age suggests that \(d\) changes with the make-up of the sample as well as with the EEG.

\textbf{Kiarashi et al.} \cite{kiarashi_sleep-derived_2025}. \emph{Research goal:} tell older adults with mild cognitive impairment (24 people) apart from cognitively normal older adults (19 people), using sleep EEG recorded at home over several nights with a device worn in the ear. A classifier gives each person a probability of mild cognitive impairment, and Cohen's \(d\) measures how far the probabilities of the two groups lie apart. Averaging over all recorded nights, instead of using the first night alone, raises \(d\) for all three neural networks tested, for example from 0.65 to 0.96. Kiarashi et al.\ attribute the rise to the averaging, which reduces the night-to-night variation within each person while keeping the differences between people. Kiarashi et al.\ also compute \(d\) for single EEG features between the two groups. The study is a preprint.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} In Henao Isaza et al.\ and Kiarashi et al., Cohen's \(d\) compares two groups of different people, with one value per person, and \(d\) changes with the people in the sample and with the variation within each person. In the present thesis, \texttt{STAY} windows and \texttt{SKIP} windows come from the same participants, yet Cohen's \(d\) is computed with the two-group version over the windows of all participants together, instead of with the version for two measurements from the same people; the baseline z-scoring of each participant, which expresses each value in standard deviations from the resting baseline, reduces the differences between participants (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_preprocessing_pipeline}), but part of the differences may remain in the standard deviation and keep \(d\) small. The raw features, such as TP10 raw standard deviation, are not z-scored at all (Section~\ref{sec:method_baseline}), so for the raw features the differences between participants remain unreduced. The share of \texttt{SKIP} windows also differs between participants (Figure~\ref{fig:classes_imbalanced}), so for the raw features the differences between participants may also shift the two class averages, and so may raise \(|d|\). The present thesis uses Cohen's \(d\) to compare the seconds before a self-paced swipe (\texttt{SKIP}) with sustained viewing (\texttt{STAY}), both to rank single features (Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_separation_cohen_d}) and to compare values of the labeling parameters (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}).

\section{Estimating Frequency from Local Maxima}
\label{sec:lit_peak_frequency}
% P14/P15: quotes and claims verified against the source PDFs of hjorth_eeg_1970, khambampati_frequency_2023 and niknazar_new_2018
Peak Frequency, one feature of the present thesis, counts per second how often the strength of one frequency band peaks (Section~\ref{sec:method_peak_frequency}). Each peak is a local maximum, a point higher than both neighbouring points. The count need not equal the frequency of the oscillation within the band. In EEG research, ``peak frequency'' often names the frequency with the largest value in the spectrum, meaning the strength of each frequency in the signal; in the present thesis, Peak Frequency names the count of local maxima per second. Three works estimate frequency, or information about frequency, from the signal over time.

\textbf{Hjorth} \cite{hjorth_eeg_1970}. \emph{Research goal:} describe the general character of an EEG trace with a few numbers, computed from the signal over time. One of the numbers is Mobility. Mobility is the square root of the ratio between the variance of the first derivative, meaning the slope of the signal, and the variance of the signal. Hjorth shows that Mobility equals the standard deviation of the spectrum along the frequency axis, and reads Mobility as a mean frequency. Computing Mobility from the signal over time needs no spectrum. Hjorth also notes that measuring the time between zero crossings, the moments the signal crosses zero, usually ends up as a frequency measure. \emph{Limits:} Hjorth links Mobility to frequency by a mathematical derivation, and tests the link on no EEG recording. The only recording shown covers one night of sleep, and plots a mean frequency that Hjorth calls very similar to Mobility, and not Mobility itself.

\textbf{Khambampati et al.} \cite{khambampati_frequency_2023}. \emph{Research goal:} predict the recovery of patients in a coma after cardiac arrest from EEG, as an entry to a public competition, the PhysioNet Challenge 2023. Khambampati et al.\ compute three features from the spectrum, among them the dominant frequency, the frequency with the largest value in the spectrum. Khambampati et al.\ compute three features from the signal over time, among them the zero-crossing rate, the number of times the signal crosses zero. In a pure sine wave, each cycle crosses zero twice. Khambampati et al.\ describe the zero-crossing rate as information about the frequency of rapid changes in the EEG. A classifier, XGBoost, combines all features with patient data such as age, and predicts the outcome with 81\% accuracy on the hidden test data. \emph{Limits:} the zero-crossing rate is one of many features. The paper reports no separate contribution of the zero-crossing rate, and does not compare the zero-crossing rate with the frequency of the signal.

\textbf{Niknazar et al.} \cite{niknazar_new_2018}. \emph{Research goal:} measure how similar two nonlinear signals are, such as signals of chaotic systems, from the local extrema of the signals. A local extremum is a local maximum or a local minimum. Niknazar et al.\ use the height of each local extremum and the time between consecutive local extrema. Niknazar et al.\ state that local extrema carry information about the amplitude and the ``global frequency'' of a signal, and treat local extrema as a down-sampled version of the signal. Niknazar et al.\ also state that finding local extrema is highly sensitive to noise, especially high-frequency noise. Added noise raises the number of local extrema. On a public EEG dataset, the method of Niknazar et al.\ classifies seizure EEG, seizure-free EEG of patients, and EEG of healthy people in five tasks, with 90\% to 97\% accuracy depending on the task, and 99\% to 100\% when a second classifier is added. \emph{Limits:} Niknazar et al.\ test the whole similarity method, and do not test the claim about frequency on its own.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} The three works count maxima, extrema or zero crossings of the signal itself. Shin et al.\ count local maxima of beta power above a threshold, and report the count per trial as the rate of beta events \cite{shin_rate_2017}. To the author's knowledge, no study counts every local maximum of the strength of an EEG band, without a threshold, so Peak Frequency is a feature the present thesis defines without prior validation. Because added noise raises the number of local extrema \cite{niknazar_new_2018}, the present thesis computes Peak Frequency within each frequency band, where filtering removes noise outside the band. The present thesis tests only how well Peak Frequency separates \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP}, with Cohen's \(d\) (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}) and with logistic regression (Section~\ref{sec:results_alternative_index}).

\section{EMG Artifacts in EEG}
\label{sec:lit_emg}
Muscles produce electrical activity of their own. A recording of muscle activity is called an electromyogram (EMG). A scalp electrode records the EMG of nearby muscles together with the EEG of the brain. EMG and EEG share many frequencies, much like two singers who sing the same notes: a filter that keeps some frequencies and removes others cannot cleanly pull the two voices apart. Two head muscles are named among the main sources of EMG in the EEG \cite{goncharova_emg_2003, yilmaz_facial_2019}: the frontalis, the forehead muscle that raises the eyebrows, and the temporalis, the muscle at the temple that closes the jaw. Four studies describe how EMG from head muscles enters the EEG, and how much EMG a cleaning method removes.

\textbf{Goncharova et al.} \cite{goncharova_emg_2003}. \emph{Research goal:} describe which frequencies and which scalp sites the EMG of the frontalis and the temporalis reaches. 25 healthy adults wore a 64-electrode cap. Participants either relaxed or tensed one muscle at a set strength, for example at 15\% of the strongest possible tension. \emph{Result:} EMG covered all frequencies from 0 to more than 200~Hz. Near the tensed muscle, EMG reached every EEG band, delta included. EMG was strongest at the electrodes closest to the tensed muscle, and spread over the whole scalp at higher frequencies. For the temporalis, EMG at temporal and nearby electrodes was highest at 40--80~Hz. On electrodes placed over the muscles themselves, EMG often showed a sharp peak that resembles an EEG beta rhythm, at 16 to 38~Hz for the frontalis and at 13 to 34~Hz for the temporalis, depending on the person. For the frontalis, the strongest EMG lay at the front edge of the scalp in two thirds of participants, usually at Fp1 or AF8. \emph{Limits:} participants tensed each muscle on purpose, guided by feedback on a screen.

\textbf{Whitham et al.} \cite{whitham_scalp_2007}. \emph{Research goal:} measure how much of the scalp EEG comes from muscles. Two men were recorded with 115 EEG channels twice: once normally, and once under a drug that paralysed all skeletal muscles except one arm, kept free for answering. Paralysis removes EMG, so the difference between the normal and the paralysed recording shows the share of EMG. \emph{Result:} under paralysis, power above 20~Hz fell, by about 100-fold at 40--100~Hz. The largest drop, above 200-fold, lay at the lower edge of the scalp, near the frontalis, the temporalis and the neck muscles. Below 20~Hz, the normal and the paralysed recording did not differ. Whitham et al.\ conclude that most scalp EEG above 20~Hz comes from muscles, even in a relaxed person. \emph{Limits:} the study has two participants. Whitham et al.\ note that the participants were highly alert during the procedure, which may have raised muscle activity, so the reported share of EMG may be on the high side.

\textbf{Yilmaz et al.} \cite{yilmaz_facial_2019}. \emph{Research goal:} test whether head muscles send EMG into the EEG during rest, and whether independent component analysis (ICA) removes the EMG. ICA is a common cleaning method: ICA splits the EEG into parts, and the parts judged to come from muscles or eyes are removed. In 13 healthy adults, fine wires inside the frontalis recorded single motor units, the smallest units of a muscle that fire together, while a 32-electrode cap recorded the EEG. For comparison, Yilmaz et al.\ reused recordings of the temporalis from an earlier study. \emph{Result:} motor units of the frontalis fired continuously while participants rested. Averaged over many firings, many motor units left a small trace on nearby frontal electrodes, mostly Fp1, Fp2, F3 and F4. Traces from the temporalis spread wider, and were strongest at F7 or Fp1. At most electrodes, ICA made the traces of both muscles significantly smaller, yet did not remove the traces completely. \emph{Limits:} the study covers two muscles, and ICA was tested only on the 19 of 58 frontalis motor units with a clear trace on more than one electrode.

\textbf{Li et al.} \cite{li_electromyogram_2021}. \emph{Research goal:} remove more EMG by adding EMG recorded from head and neck muscles as extra input to ICA. \emph{Result:} in 8 healthy adults clenching one hand, the method removed about 75\% of the power at 40--100~Hz, a range dominated by EMG, where ICA alone removed about 49\%. \emph{Limits:} the method still cannot remove all EMG, partly because some head and neck muscles are hard to record from the skin.

\textbf{What the four studies show together.} Whitham et al.\ and Yilmaz et al.\ find EMG even at rest. Below 20~Hz, Goncharova et al.\ show EMG near a tensed muscle, while Whitham et al.\ found no difference below 20~Hz between the normal and the paralysed recording. ICA, with or without extra EMG input, reduces EMG without removing EMG completely.

The Muse~S of the present thesis records at AF7 and AF8 on the forehead, near the frontalis, where Goncharova et al.\ and Yilmaz et al.\ found the strongest EMG, and at TP9 and TP10 behind the ears, at the lower edge of the scalp next to the small muscles around the ear \cite{mcmenamin_electromyogenic_2011}, where Whitham et al.\ found the largest share of EMG. The present thesis applies no EMG correction, records no extra EMG, and removes no windows flagged as artifacts (Section~\ref{sec:method_artifact_analysis}). Facial muscle activity changes with mental effort and emotion \cite{mcmenamin_electromyogenic_2011}, so if jaw or forehead tension changes before a swipe, the three features the present thesis tests further, TP10 raw standard deviation, AF7 high-gamma mean and AF8 delta peak frequency (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}), may reflect muscle activity, brain activity, or both. The present thesis cannot tell muscle activity and brain activity apart, and returns to the origin of the three features in Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}.

\section{Delta-Band Function in Awake Cognition}
\label{sec:lit_delta}
Delta is the slowest EEG band. The present thesis defines delta as 1--4~Hz (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_preprocessing_pipeline}). Delta is best known from deep sleep. Two reviews describe what delta does in the awake brain, and one study relates delta to addiction to short-video apps.

\textbf{Güntekin and Başar} \cite{guntekin_review_2016}. \emph{Research goal:} review delta responses in healthy people and in several disorders. A delta response is a change in delta right after a stimulus, such as a tone or a picture. \emph{Result:} delta responses take part in cognitive processes, mainly in decision making and attention, and in emotional processes. Delta responses are smaller in Alzheimer's disease, mild cognitive impairment, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia and alcoholism. Güntekin and Başar therefore propose a smaller delta response as a possible general marker of impaired cognition. \emph{Limits:} the review mainly covers delta right after a stimulus.

\textbf{Knyazev} \cite{knyazev_eeg_2012}. \emph{Research goal:} explain what delta does, from the view of evolution. \emph{Result:} delta dominates the waking EEG of reptiles. In humans, delta is strong mainly early in development and in deep sleep. Delta rises with hunger, sexual arousal and pain, and in drug users, which links delta to motivation. In cognition, delta is linked to attention, to salience detection, meaning the noticing of what matters, and to perception below awareness. Knyazev concludes that delta reflects old, basic processes. In awake adults, newer processes tied to faster bands overshadow the old processes. The old processes rise when the newer processes lose priority, for example in sleep, early in development, or in disease. \emph{Limits:} the conclusion is a hypothesis built on a review, and Knyazev notes that the evidence is scarce.

\textbf{Xu et al.} \cite{xu_neural_2024}. \emph{Research goal:} test whether addiction to short-video apps goes with weaker working memory and weaker cognitive control in adolescents. Cognitive control was tested as the switching between two tasks. \emph{Design:} 84 Chinese adolescents (mean age 12.3) filled in an addiction questionnaire and did two cognitive tests. One week later, an 8-electrode cap recorded 1 minute of rest and then 3 minutes of free browsing on Douyin, the Chinese version of TikTok. Xu et al.\ compared the power of each band during browsing with the power at rest. \emph{Result:} delta rose over the whole head during browsing, which Xu et al.\ read as attention to the videos. Of all bands, only the change in delta predicted the addiction score ($F=2.184$, $p=0.040$, adjusted $R^2=0.115$). The more addicted an adolescent, the less delta rose at left temporal and left occipital electrodes. The delta pattern tied to addiction predicted lower working memory ($r=-0.226$, $p=0.043$) and lower cognitive control ($r=-0.220$, $p=0.047$). \emph{Limits:} the effects are small, browsing lasted 3 minutes, and the paper reports no times of single swipes.

\textbf{What the three works show together.} Güntekin and Başar link delta right after a stimulus to attention and decision making. Knyazev links delta to motivation, attention and salience detection, drawing also on delta at rest and in sleep. Xu et al.\ link the change in delta during free browsing to a questionnaire score of addiction.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} None of the three works relates delta to the seconds before a self-paced swipe: Güntekin and Başar review delta mainly right after a stimulus, and Xu et al.\ report no times of single swipes. The three works also study mainly the strength of delta, whereas AF8 delta peak frequency, one of the three features the present thesis tests further (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}), counts how often per second the strength of the delta band peaks (Section~\ref{sec:method_peak_frequency}). The present thesis tests how well the delta features, AF8 delta peak frequency among them, separate the seconds before each swipe (\texttt{SKIP}) from sustained viewing (\texttt{STAY}), and compares the delta features with the features of the other bands and with the EI (Sections~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance} and~\ref{sec:results_alternative_index}).

\section{Notch Filtering and the High-Gamma Band}
\label{sec:lit_notch_high_gamma}
The recordings of the present thesis took place in Germany (Section~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}). Power lines in Germany carry alternating current at 50~Hz. EEG electrodes pick up the electrical field of the power lines as powerline-noise, a sharp peak at 50~Hz. A notch filter removes a narrow band of frequencies around 50~Hz. The present thesis defines high gamma as 40--60~Hz (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_preprocessing_pipeline}). The 50~Hz peak therefore lies in the middle of high gamma. A notch filter removes powerline-noise and any brain activity near 50~Hz together. One study compares a low-cost EEG amplifier with a research amplifier in the gamma range.

\textbf{Pattisapu and Ray} \cite{pattisapu_stimulus-induced_2023}. \emph{Research goal:} test whether a low-cost, open-source EEG amplifier (OpenBCI) records gamma as well as a research amplifier (Brain Products). \emph{Design:} 11 adults looked at full-screen striped patterns. Full-screen striped patterns are known to raise gamma. Each participant was recorded in two sessions, once with each amplifier. Both amplifiers recorded through the same cap of gel electrodes. \emph{Result:} OpenBCI recorded a gamma response, a rise in gamma power from before the patterns to during the patterns, in almost all participants who showed a gamma response with the research amplifier. The alpha responses and gamma responses of the two amplifiers were highly correlated. In a test with the electrodes in salt water and no person attached, OpenBCI showed more powerline-noise than the research amplifier. OpenBCI also showed three extra peaks, at 14, 36 and 64~Hz. 36 and 64~Hz lie 14~Hz below and above 50~Hz. Pattisapu and Ray trace the extra peaks to a distortion of the powerline-noise inside the OpenBCI amplifier. The research amplifier showed no extra peaks. In the recordings of people, the extra peaks remained in some participants. The gamma response was smaller with OpenBCI, especially in fast gamma (35--66~Hz). Pattisapu and Ray name the extra peaks as one possible reason. \emph{Limits:} the study used gel electrodes, unlike the dry electrodes of the Muse~S.

In the bands of the present thesis, the three extra peaks would fall into beta (14~Hz), low gamma (36~Hz) and very high gamma (64~Hz). The present thesis did not test whether the Muse~S shows the same extra peaks. To the author's knowledge, no study measures for a consumer headband how much of high gamma is powerline-noise and how much is brain activity. The present thesis applies no notch filter in any reported analysis. A notch-filtered copy serves only the artifact check (Section~\ref{sec:method_artifact_analysis}). Two of the three features the present thesis tests further may therefore partly reflect powerline-noise: AF7 high-gamma mean, and TP10 raw standard deviation, computed on the raw signal, the signal before the split into bands, which keeps the 50~Hz peak. If the Muse~S shows the same extra peaks, beta, low-gamma and very-high-gamma results may also partly reflect the extra peaks (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}). Very high gamma (60--100~Hz) also reaches 100~Hz, twice 50~Hz, where powerline-noise can show a second peak.

\section{Pre-Motor Window}
\label{sec:lit_premotor}
The brain prepares a voluntary movement before the movement starts, and the muscles activate shortly before the movement. EEG recorded shortly before a movement may therefore partly reflect the preparation of the movement. Three works give the timing of brain and muscle activity before a self-paced movement.

\textbf{Libet et al.} \cite{libet_time_1983}. \emph{Research goal:} compare the start of brain activity before a voluntary movement with the moment a person becomes aware of wanting to move. \emph{Design:} 5 participants flexed the fingers or the wrist of the right hand whenever they felt like it. Libet et al.\ measure all times from the start of muscle activity in the forearm, which comes just before the movement. Participants watched a spot circling like a clock hand. After each movement, participants reported where the spot had been when the wish to move first appeared. \emph{Result:} the brain activity before the movement is called the \emph{readiness potential}, or Bereitschaftspotential. The readiness potential is a slow negative shift in the EEG. In series without planning ahead, the readiness potential began on average about 550~ms before the muscle activity. In series where participants sometimes planned ahead, the readiness potential began on average about 1{,}025~ms before the muscle activity. The reported wish to move came about 200~ms before the muscle activity, so the readiness potential began about 350~ms before the reported wish without planning ahead, and about 800~ms before the reported wish with some planning ahead. Libet et al.\ conclude that the brain can start a voluntary movement before the person is aware of wanting to move. \emph{Limits:} the study has 5 participants, the moment of awareness rests on what each participant recalled, and each EEG recording covered only 1.4~s before the muscle activity, so an earlier start of the readiness potential could not be seen.

\textbf{Shakeel et al.} \cite{shakeel_review_2015}. \emph{Research goal:} review methods that detect from EEG that a person is about to move. \emph{Result:} the readiness potential begins about 1 to 1.5~s before the movement. The readiness potential has an early part, which rises slowly, and a late part, which begins about 400--500~ms before the movement and rises more steeply. The start of the readiness potential varies considerably between conditions and between people. The summary of the review gives a start of about 2~s before the movement. \emph{Limits:} the timing values come from earlier studies, and the review measures none of them.

\textbf{Lew et al.} \cite{lew_detection_2012}. \emph{Research goal:} detect from EEG, in single trials, that a person is about to start a self-paced reaching movement. \emph{Design:} in the first experiment, 8 healthy adults rested one hand on a home button. A sound named one of four targets. Participants then reached for the target whenever they wished, but not earlier than 2~s after the sound. Release of the home button marked the start of the movement. Electrodes on the skin recorded the EMG of the biceps, the muscle at the front of the upper arm. \emph{Result:} the biceps activated on average 263~$\pm$~40~ms before the start of the movement. A classifier detected the coming movement from the EEG above chance, on average about 460~ms before the start of the movement. \emph{Limits:} the movement is an arm reach, and a sound set the target and the earliest start.

\textbf{What the three works show together.} The reported start of the readiness potential ranges from about 0.55~s before the movement in Libet et al.\ to about 2~s in the summary of Shakeel et al., and varies between people. All three works measure time from the movement, or from the muscle activity just before the movement.

The present thesis leaves the \emph{pre-swipe gap}, the last 2.0~s before each keypress that marks a swipe, out of both \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} (Section~\ref{sec:op_labeling_params}). The participant presses the key with the other hand at the moment of each swipe (Sections~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition} and~\ref{sec:method_experimental_protocol}), so the brain likely prepares two movements, the swipe and the keypress. If the keypress comes slightly before or after the swipe, the true gap before the swipe is slightly shorter or longer than 2.0~s. The pre-swipe gap exceeds the start of about 1 to 1.5~s in Shakeel et al.\ by about 0.5 to 1~s, and leaves no margin to the start of about 2~s in the summary of Shakeel et al. The present thesis does not test whether the \texttt{SKIP} windows are free of movement preparation. In bursts, some \texttt{SKIP} windows even lie inside the pre-swipe gap of a neighbouring swipe (Section~\ref{sec:op_stay_skip}).


\section{Summary of Gaps}
\label{sec:lit_gaps}
To the author's knowledge, the reviewed literature leaves seven gaps:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item No EEG study anchors a label to the self-paced swipe (Sections~\ref{sec:adjacent_studies} and~\ref{sec:secondary_adjacent_studies}).
    \item Engagement lacks an agreed definition (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definitions}).
    \item Research measures engagement mostly by time spent and counts. Whether long viewing means engagement is untested (Sections~\ref{sec:health_communication_literature} and~\ref{sec:engagement_short_form}).
    \item No health communication study records a body-route signal before a viewer swipes away (Section~\ref{sec:health_communication_literature}).
    \item No study reports the EI before a self-paced decision (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definitions}).
    \item No study varies window timing or label borders before a swipe (Sections~\ref{sec:lit_parameter_choice} and~\ref{sec:lit_label_borders}).
    \item For a consumer headband, the share of powerline-noise in high gamma is unmeasured (Section~\ref{sec:lit_notch_high_gamma}).
\end{enumerate}


