\section{Data Acquisition}
\label{sec:method_data_acquisition}
EEG was recorded with a consumer-grade Muse~S headband (Gen~2, Figure~\ref{fig:muse_headband}). The Muse~S has four dry electrodes, which touch the skin without gel. Two electrodes sit on the forehead (AF7, AF8), and two sit behind the ears (TP9, TP10). The labels follow the international 10--20 system (Section~\ref{sec:adjacent_studies}). Each electrode measures voltage against a reference electrode at Fpz, the middle of the forehead \cite{moontaha_online_2023}. The Muse~S samples at 256~Hz \cite{rahman_detection_2025}. Two units of the same model were used across participants. Differences between the two units therefore cannot be fully separated from differences between participants (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}). All recordings took place in Germany.

Earlier Muse models have been tested in research. Krigolson et al.\ tested the 2014 Muse, which uses the same electrode sites \cite{krigolson_choosing_2017}. In two tasks, the 2014 Muse recorded three event-related potentials (ERPs): the N200 and the P300, named for their polarity (negative, positive) and latency (about 200 and 300~ms), and the reward positivity. An ERP is the brain response to a stimulus, averaged over many repeats. The Muse ERPs closely matched those of a 64-electrode research system, once the research data were reduced to the Muse electrode sites and reference. Against the full research analysis, the N200 and the P300 had reversed sign. The test covers only frequencies below 15~Hz. Moontaha et al.\ used the Muse~S Gen~1 to classify emotions during film clips \cite{moontaha_online_2023}. A systematic review of EEG-based user engagement research lists the Muse in two of the reviewed studies \cite{bellos_methods_2025}.

