Participants first received an information sheet. Participants then signed the consent and data protection forms (Section~\ref{sec:method_participants}) and filled in the pre-session survey. The pre-session survey took about five to ten minutes.

The author (experiment lead) then fitted the Muse~S headband, adjusted the headband for good skin contact on the forehead and behind the ears, especially for participants with long hair, and checked the signal quality and the battery charge.

The session had two phases: the \emph{baseline phase} and the \emph{browsing phase}.

In the baseline phase, participants held the author's iPhone, which played a two-minute nature video on YouTube. The author asked participants to relax and simply watch the video. Guided Access, an iPhone setting, kept the iPhone in the open app. The author started the recording pipeline, pressed the ``B'' key, and left the space, so that participants had privacy. After two minutes, Guided Access timed out on screen. The time-out ended the baseline phase.

The author then returned and explained the browsing phase. Before every participant, the author reset the TikTok ``For You'' feed with the ``Reset feed'' option in the content preferences. Participants were asked to use TikTok as usual: to watch videos they found interesting, and to swipe away videos they found boring. Participants were asked not to like, comment or visit profiles, so that the swipe stayed the only action. The browsing phase is naturalistic use (Section~\ref{sec:adjacent_studies}): self-paced viewing of an algorithmic feed, with a swipe possible at any moment.

Free viewing has been used in brain imaging before. In one fMRI study, five people freely watched 30 minutes of one film. Eye movements were free, and the film ran without breaks. Brain activity still correlated significantly across the five people, over large parts of the cortex \cite{hasson_intersubject_2004}. Nobody in that study could skip.

In the present study's experiment, participants were asked to press the ``A'' key at the same moment as every swipe (Section~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}, Figure~\ref{fig:keypress_sync}).

