\section{Labeling Strategy}
\label{sec:method_skipstay_labeling_strategy}
Four labeling parameters define the \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} labels: the pre-swipe gap, the SKIP period, the post-swipe gap and the window length. The present section sets the four labeling parameters, defines \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP}, and checks how much the separation between \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} depends on three of the four labeling parameters.

\subsection{Operational Definition of Labeling Parameters}
\label{sec:op_labeling_params}
Around each swipe, time runs in this order: STAY period, SKIP period, pre-swipe gap, ``A'' keypress, post-swipe gap, STAY period (Figure~\ref{fig:labeling_strategy}). The pre-swipe gap, the SKIP period and the post-swipe gap count from the ``A'' keypress that marks a swipe (Section~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}).

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Pre-swipe gap: 2.0~s.} The last 2.0~s before each keypress belong to neither label. The pre-swipe gap is meant to keep the preparation of the swipe out of \texttt{SKIP}. The readiness potential, a slow EEG shift before a voluntary movement, starts about 1 to 1.5~s before the movement \cite{shakeel_review_2015}. Arm muscles activate on average 263~$\pm$~40~ms before a self-paced reach \cite{lew_detection_2012}. The 2.0~s are longer than the 1 to 1.5~s and longer than the 263~ms. The summary of the review by Shakeel et al.\ gives a start of about 2~s, and the start differs between people. The definition has a limit: in a person whose readiness potential starts earlier than 2~s before the keypress, the last \texttt{SKIP} window may already hold the start of the readiness potential (Section~\ref{sec:lit_premotor}).
    \item \textbf{SKIP period: 3.0~s.} \texttt{SKIP} windows come from the 3.0~s just before the pre-swipe gap, from 5~s to 2~s before each keypress. To the author's knowledge, no study gives a value for this period. The 3.0~s serve as a starting point, varied exploratively in Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}.
    \item \textbf{Post-swipe gap: 0.5~s.} The first 0.5~s after each keypress belong to neither label. The post-swipe gap is meant to keep the end of the swipe movement, and the first moment of the new video, out of \texttt{STAY}. The post-swipe gap is not varied in Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}.
    \item \textbf{Window length: 1.0~s.} The SKIP period and the STAY period are cut into analysis windows of 1.0~s. In a test of 1 to 5~s windows on wearable EEG, the shortest window, 1~s, scored best \cite{moontaha_online_2023}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Operational Definition of STAY and SKIP}
\label{sec:op_stay_skip}
\texttt{SKIP} windows come from the SKIP period. \texttt{STAY} windows come from the STAY period. The STAY period is simply all remaining time of the browsing phase that does not belong to any other gap or period. In other words, the STAY period starts after a post-swipe gap and ends where the next SKIP period starts. With the default values, each SKIP period with default length 3.0~s holds three 1.0~s \texttt{SKIP} windows.

\texttt{STAY} captures engagement only in part. Long viewing without a swipe can also be viewing without attention (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_short_form}).

Swipes often come in bursts (Section~\ref{sec:method_behavioral_burst_analysis}). With the default values, when two swipes lie less than 5.5~s apart, the SKIP period of the later swipe reaches into the pre-swipe gap or the post-swipe gap of the earlier swipe. The overlapping time then counts as \texttt{SKIP}. Figure~\ref{fig:labeling_strategy_small_window} shows an example near 15~s. Two keypresses lie only 1.2~s apart, so the SKIP periods of the two swipes merge into one SKIP period of about 4.2~s. The merged SKIP period holds four \texttt{SKIP} windows instead of three. Figure~\ref{fig:labeling_strategy_small_window} uses 0.3~s windows instead of the default 1.0~s, so each \texttt{SKIP} window shows as a separate dash. In Figure~\ref{fig:labeling_strategy}, the same four \texttt{SKIP} windows join into one bar. Of all 9,307 \texttt{SKIP} windows, 2,179 (23\%; 0 to 44\% per participant) lie within the pre-swipe gap or the post-swipe gap of a neighbouring swipe. These \texttt{SKIP} windows may therefore contain preparation or movement of the neighbouring swipe. The present thesis keeps these \texttt{SKIP} windows (Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}).

