\subsection{Baseline and Border Exclusion}
The baseline phase gives no labels. The baseline phase serves only to normalise the signal of each participant (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_preprocessing_pipeline}). The classifiers therefore separate \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP} within the browsing phase only.

The STAY period and the SKIP period meet where the SKIP period starts: with the default values, 5~s before the keypress. No window crosses this border (Section~\ref{sec:method_windowing}). Each window therefore carries one label only. Moontaha et al.\ likewise use only windows that lie completely inside one video \cite{moontaha_online_2023}.

Windows right next to the border stay in the analysis, although the moment the decision to swipe begins is unknown (Section~\ref{sec:lit_label_borders}). If the decision begins 7~s before the keypress, the \texttt{STAY} windows from 7~s to 5~s already hold the decision. If the decision begins 3~s before the keypress, the \texttt{SKIP} windows from 5~s to 3~s do not yet hold the decision. The sensitivity analysis moves the border by changing the pre-swipe gap and the SKIP period (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Class Separation Metric}
\label{sec:method_class_separation_metric}
Cohen's \(d\) measures how far the \texttt{STAY} windows and the \texttt{SKIP} windows lie apart in one feature, in units of the standard deviation of the feature (Section~\ref{sec:lit_class_separation}). A larger \(|d|\), the size of Cohen's \(d\), means a stronger separation. It means the feature with a larger \(|d|\) distinguishes both the classes \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} better from one another than a feature with a smaller \(|d|\). The denominator is the square root of the average of the two class variances, the version Cohen gives for two groups that spread differently \cite{cohen_statistical_1988}:

\begin{equation}
    d = \frac{\bar{x}_{\text{STAY}} - \bar{x}_{\text{SKIP}}}{\sqrt{\frac{s_{\text{STAY}}^2 + s_{\text{SKIP}}^2}{2}}}
    \label{eq:cohens_d}
\end{equation}
where \(\bar{x}_{\text{STAY}}\) and \(\bar{x}_{\text{SKIP}}\) denote the means of the feature over all \texttt{STAY} windows and over all \texttt{SKIP} windows, and \(s_{\text{STAY}}^2\) and \(s_{\text{SKIP}}^2\) the corresponding variances.

Cohen's \(d\) is computed once for every feature (e.g.\ TP10 raw standard deviation), over the windows of all participants together. Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_separation_cohen_d} describes the two levels at which \(|d|\) is reported: exact values per feature, and means per category.

\subsection{Parameter Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}
The sensitivity analysis is exploratory and rough: the sensitivity analysis shows where the default values sit among nearby values, and points to future research. The sensitivity analysis therefore belongs to the Methods chapter, and the start of Chapter~\ref{chap:results} lists the result among the findings reported in the Methods chapter.

The sensitivity analysis asks how much the separation between \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} depends on three labeling parameters: the pre-swipe gap, the SKIP period and the window length. The post-swipe gap stays at 0.5~s. Each tested setting reruns the pipeline from labeling to features. Each setting then yields one number: the largest \(|d|\) of any single feature out of 236, over the windows of all participants.

Crossing the three labeling parameters, with four values each, would need \(4 \times 4 \times 4 = 64\) pipeline runs. The main analysis needs the labeling parameters before the main analysis can start, so two less intensive designs were used, with 19 pipeline runs for basic exploration:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Pre-swipe gap and SKIP period, crossed.} Every pre-swipe gap (0.5, 1, 2 and 3~s) was combined with every SKIP period (2, 3, 4 and 6~s), with the window length fixed at 1.0~s: 16 runs.
    \item \textbf{Window length, alone.} The window length (0.3, 0.7, 1.0 and 2.0~s) was varied with the pre-swipe gap fixed at 2.0~s and the SKIP period at 3.0~s: 3 further runs, because the 1.0~s run is part of the grid. Changing one labeling parameter while the others stay fixed is the OAT design of Section~\ref{sec:lit_parameter_choice} \cite[\S2.4.2]{saltelli_global_2008}. The stride, the step from one window start to the next, stayed at 1.0~s. The 2.0~s windows therefore overlap by half, and the 0.3~s windows leave 0.7~s of every second unused.
\end{itemize}

The sensitivity analysis has three limits. First, the OAT design cannot show an interaction (Section~\ref{sec:lit_parameter_choice}): whether the effect of the window length depends on the pre-swipe gap or on the SKIP period \cite{saltelli_global_2008}. The window-length result therefore holds only near the fixed values. Second, the grid shows the joint effect of pre-swipe gap and SKIP period without a statistical test. Third, the sensitivity analysis pools all participants, so the sensitivity analysis cannot show a best value per person, as Mi et al.\ found for window length \cite{mi_exploring_2026}. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} lists the three limits.

Figures~\ref{fig:labeling_strategy} to~\ref{fig:labeling_strategy_small_gap_big_isp} show the same 70~s of participant P4 under three settings. Blue bars are \texttt{STAY} windows, red bars \texttt{SKIP} windows, shaded red areas SKIP periods, and vertical red lines keypresses. Figure~\ref{fig:labeling_strategy} shows the default values. In Figure~\ref{fig:labeling_strategy_small_window}, the window length drops from 1.0~s to 0.3~s, so the bars turn into short dashes with spaces between them. In Figure~\ref{fig:labeling_strategy_small_gap_big_isp}, the pre-swipe gap shrinks from 2.0~s to 0.5~s, so the white space before each keypress narrows, and the SKIP period grows from 3.0~s to 4.0~s, so the shaded areas widen.

