\section{Reproducible Recording Pipeline}
\label{sec:method_universal_recording_pipeline}
The reproducible recording pipeline engineered by the author, records the EEG of the Muse~S and the keypresses of each session. The recording pipeline is one Python file. The file is public at \url{https://github.com/coffeeandcoffee/eeg_tiktok_pipeline/blob/main/data_collection/recording_script.py}. The recording pipeline can also record webcam video. The present thesis recorded no video (Section~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}).

Each session started the recording pipeline with one command:
\begin{center}
\verb|python recording_script.py --nocamera --duration 1800|
\end{center}
The flag \verb|--nocamera| switches the webcam off. The flag \verb|--duration 1800| stops the recording after 1,800~s, that is, after 30 minutes.

The recording pipeline runs in five steps:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Find the headband.} The recording pipeline scans for Muse headbands over Bluetooth and lists every Muse found. The author picks one Muse from the list, so no device address has to be written into the code.
    \item \textbf{Start the stream.} The recording pipeline starts \texttt{muselsl stream} (Section~\ref{sec:libraries}). \texttt{muselsl} passes the EEG into LSL. The recording pipeline reads the EEG from LSL at 256~Hz, one sample at a time, with the Python library \texttt{pylsl}.
    \item \textbf{Mark the keypresses.} A keypress listener, built on the Python library \texttt{pynput}, stamps every ``A'' keypress of the participant and every ``B'' keypress of the author with the laptop clock. At the first EEG sample of each connection, the recording pipeline measures the difference between the laptop clock and LSL time once. With this difference, the recording pipeline converts each keypress time into LSL time. The first EEG sample within 100~ms of the keypress gets a flag: 1 in the column for ``A'' or ``B'', 0 otherwise. The flag sits in the same row as the EEG sample, in the recording file.
    \item \textbf{Reconnect after a Bluetooth dropout.} Bluetooth connections can drop. When no EEG sample arrives for 5~s, the recording pipeline stops \texttt{muselsl}, scans for the headband again, restarts the stream, and continues in a new file (\texttt{\_2.csv}, \texttt{\_3.csv}, and so on). The EEG during a Bluetooth dropout is lost. Earlier files stay intact.
    \item \textbf{Keep reading while listening.} EEG reading and the keypress listener each run in a thread of their own. EEG reading does not wait for the keypress listener, and the keypress listener does not wait for EEG reading.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Reproducible Preprocessing Pipeline}
\label{sec:method_universal_preprocessing_pipeline}

The reproducible preprocessing pipeline engineered by the author, turns the recording files of the recording pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_recording_pipeline}) into labeled analysis windows. The code is public at \url{https://github.com/coffeeandcoffee/eeg_tiktok_pipeline}. The preprocessing pipeline runs twice: once without and once with a 50~Hz notch filter. Only the artifact check uses the notch-filtered copy (Section~\ref{sec:method_artifact_analysis}).

\subsection{Data Synchronization and Interpolation}
\label{sec:method_sync}
Each Bluetooth dropout ends one recording file and starts the next recording file (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_recording_pipeline}). The preprocessing pipeline processes each recording file on its own and never joins two recording files (executed via \url{https://github.com/coffeeandcoffee/eeg_tiktok_pipeline/blob/main/data_analysis/step01_preprocess.py}). No analysis window can therefore cross a Bluetooth dropout.

The preprocessing pipeline keeps each recording file from 5.0~s before the first ``A'' keypress in the recording file to 0.5~s after the last ``A'' keypress. With the default values, the kept part starts with the first SKIP period and ends with the last post-swipe gap (Section~\ref{sec:op_labeling_params}).

The raw samples arrive at slightly uneven intervals. Over the whole recording file, the raw rate averages between 255.93 and 256.02 samples per second, across all 61 recording files of the 25 participants. The band filters of Section~\ref{sec:method_baseline} assume evenly spaced samples. Linear interpolation therefore puts every recording file on an even grid of exactly 256 samples per second.

The preprocessing pipeline measures each Bluetooth dropout as the time between the last sample of one recording file and the first sample of the next recording file, and logs every Bluetooth dropout. Figure~\ref{fig:dropouts} shows the Bluetooth dropouts per participant.

