\subsection{Baseline Extraction and Normalization}
\label{sec:method_baseline}
The preprocessing pipeline splits the signal of each electrode into seven frequency bands with a 4th-order Butterworth bandpass filter: delta (1--4~Hz), theta (4--8~Hz), alpha (8--13~Hz), beta (13--30~Hz), low gamma (30--40~Hz), high gamma (40--60~Hz), and very high gamma (60--100~Hz). The undecomposed broadband signal is kept alongside the seven bands and is called the \emph{raw} signal throughout. For each band, the Hilbert transform gives the \emph{envelope} \(A\): the strength of the band at every moment, like a line traced along the tops of the waves. The envelope is not squared to power.

The baseline gives every participant, electrode and band a separate zero point, so that values of different participants can be compared. The baseline is the 90~s from 10~s to 100~s after the first ``B'' keypress of the author, within the two-minute baseline phase (Section~\ref{sec:method_experimental_protocol}). The baseline comes from the recording files before the trimming of Section~\ref{sec:method_sync}. For each band at each electrode, \(\mu_{base}\) and \(\sigma_{base}\) are the mean and the standard deviation of the envelope over the baseline. Every sample of the envelope is then z-scored:
\begin{equation}
    z = \frac{A - \mu_{base}}{\sigma_{base}}
    \label{eq:zscore}
\end{equation}
A value of \(z = 1\) means that the band is one baseline standard deviation stronger than during the baseline. The z-scoring reduces the differences between participants, but part of the differences may remain (Section~\ref{sec:lit_class_separation}).

The raw signal is not z-scored. For the raw signal, the preprocessing pipeline only subtracts the mean of each recording file. The raw features, such as TP10 raw standard deviation (Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_engineering}), therefore stay in the unit of the recording and keep the differences between participants in signal strength.

\subsection{Windowing and Class Labeling}
\label{sec:method_windowing}
After normalization, the preprocessing pipeline cuts the signal into \texttt{STAY} windows and \texttt{SKIP} windows around each ``A'' keypress, with the default values of Section~\ref{sec:op_labeling_params}: a 3.0~s SKIP period, a 2.0~s pre-swipe gap and a 0.5~s post-swipe gap (executed via \url{https://github.com/coffeeandcoffee/eeg_tiktok_pipeline/blob/main/data_analysis/step03_label_windows.py}). No window takes data from a pre-swipe gap or a post-swipe gap, except \texttt{SKIP} windows in bursts (Section~\ref{sec:op_stay_skip}).

The analysis windows last 1.0~s and do not overlap (stride = window length). Each window holds samples of one label only. At the end of a stretch, a window is kept if at least 0.8~s of the window lies inside the stretch, and then holds only the samples inside the stretch. 531 of the 27,643 windows (1.9\%) are such shortened windows at the end of a stretch.

\subsection{Artifact Analysis}
\label{sec:method_artifact_analysis}
The artifact check flags likely blinks and likely muscle or jaw movement artifacts (executed via \url{https://github.com/coffeeandcoffee/eeg_tiktok_pipeline/blob/main/data_analysis/step02_artifact_flag.py}). The artifact check removes nothing. Every flagged sample stays in every analysis of the present thesis. The artifact check only shows that artifacts are present. The missing artifact removal is a limitation of the present thesis (Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}) and is left to future research (Section~\ref{sec:future_research}).

The artifact check cuts the signal of each electrode into short segments that overlap by half:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Blinks:} AF7 and AF8, filtered to 1--40~Hz, segments of 0.5~s.
    \item \textbf{EMG:} TP9 and TP10, filtered to 30--100~Hz, segments of 0.25~s.
\end{itemize}
For each segment, the artifact check measures the peak-to-peak amplitude, the distance from the lowest point to the highest point. The artifact check flags a segment when the peak-to-peak amplitude lies above the 95th percentile of all segments of the same electrode in the same recording file.

The 95th percentile flags a fixed share of segments by design. The flagged share of samples in Figure~\ref{fig:artifact_rate}, between 7.5\% and 15\% per participant and artifact type, therefore looks alike across participants. The flagged share cannot show which participant recorded cleaner data.

The artifact check runs on the notch-filtered copy, so that the 50~Hz powerline-noise does not raise the peak-to-peak amplitude. All other analyses use the signal without a notch filter, because a notch filter would also remove any brain activity near 50~Hz, inside high gamma (Section~\ref{sec:lit_notch_high_gamma}).

EMG is strongest at electrodes near head muscles \cite{goncharova_emg_2003, whitham_scalp_2007}, such as TP9 and TP10 at the lower edge of the scalp (Section~\ref{sec:lit_emg}). AF7 and AF8 lie near the forehead muscle and pick up EMG as well (Section~\ref{sec:lit_emg}). At AF7 and AF8, however, the artifact check looks only for blinks.

