\subsection{Behavioural Burst Analysis}
\label{sec:method_behavioral_burst_analysis}

Swipes often come in quick succession. The present thesis calls several rapid swipes in a row a \emph{burst}. The \emph{inter-swipe interval} is the time between two consecutive ``A'' keypresses, roughly the viewing time of one video. For the burst analysis, each swipe gets a \emph{burst window} of $\pm$3.0~s. When two swipes lie less than 6.0~s apart, the burst windows of the two swipes overlap, and the two swipes merge into one \emph{burst block}. Figure~\ref{fig:bursts} shows the inter-swipe intervals of all participants and the 6.0~s merge threshold.

The burst window and the SKIP period are two separate definitions. The burst window only groups swipes for the burst analysis and changes no label. The SKIP period defines the \texttt{SKIP} windows (Section~\ref{sec:op_labeling_params}). Both definitions use the value 3.0~s, and the burst window and the SKIP period can overlap in time. Section~\ref{sec:op_stay_skip} describes what bursts do to the SKIP period.

The author chose the 3.0~s of the burst window without a source and without looking at the inter-swipe intervals. Section~\ref{sec:results_burst_analysis} shows how the burst blocks change with a $\pm$5.0~s burst window. The burst analysis removes nothing: bursts stay in the data. Whether bursts should be removed is left to future research (Section~\ref{sec:future_research}).

