\subsection{Engagement Index}
Pope et al.\ selected the EI to switch a computer task modelled on flight-deck work between manual and automated operation \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3} (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definitions}):
\begin{equation}
    EI = \frac{\beta}{\alpha + \theta}
    \label{eq:formula_EI}
\end{equation}
Pope et al.\ used band power, summed over the four central and parietal electrode sites Cz, Pz, P3 and P4, with beta at 13--22~Hz \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}.

The present thesis adapts the EI to the z-scored envelope. For each window, the pipeline computes the variance of the z-scored envelope of beta, alpha and theta at each of the four electrodes. The pipeline then averages each band over the four electrodes. The three averages enter Equation~\ref{eq:formula_EI}. Figure~\ref{fig:calculation_visual_EI} shows the calculation for one window.

Band power, the input of Pope et al., is never negative. The mean of a z-scored envelope compares the band with the baseline, and can be negative or close to zero. A ratio of such means can flip its sign or become very large. The variance is never negative, like band power. The variance of the z-scored envelope measures how much the band strength fluctuates within the window, relative to the baseline. The variance does not measure the level of the band strength, which band power measures. The present thesis did not test an EI built on the level of the band strength.

The EI of the present thesis also differs from the EI of Pope et al.\ in the electrodes (AF7, AF8, TP9 and TP10) and in the beta band (13--30~Hz). EI values of the present thesis are therefore not comparable with EI values of Pope et al. Section~\ref{sec:method_result_synthesis_pipeline} describes how the EI serves as the benchmark.

