\subsection{Hjorth Parameters}
Mean and standard deviation ignore the order of the samples: shuffling the samples of a window leaves the mean and the standard deviation unchanged. Hjorth parameters depend on the order of the samples, because Hjorth parameters use the slope of the signal \cite{hjorth_eeg_1970}. The pipeline takes the slope \(y'(t)\) as the change from one sample of \(y(t)\) to the next, and the change in slope \(y''(t)\) likewise.

\paragraph{Activity.} Activity is the variance of \(y(t)\), the square of the standard deviation:
\begin{equation}
    \text{Activity} = \sigma_{y}^2 = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{t=1}^{N} (y(t) - \mu_y)^2
\end{equation}
where \(\mu_y\) is the mean of \(y(t)\). The EI builds on the same variance of beta, alpha and theta.

\paragraph{Mobility.} Mobility is the standard deviation of the slope, divided by the standard deviation of \(y(t)\):
\begin{equation}
    \text{Mobility} = \frac{\sigma_{y'}}{\sigma_{y}} = \sqrt{\frac{\text{var}(y'(t))}{\text{var}(y(t))}}
\end{equation}
Hjorth reads Mobility as a mean frequency \cite{hjorth_eeg_1970}. Here, \(y(t)\) is the envelope. Mobility therefore describes how fast the band strength rises and falls, rather than the frequency of the waves within the band. The slope is taken per sample, so Mobility carries the unit per sample.

\paragraph{Complexity.} Complexity is the Mobility of the slope, divided by the Mobility of \(y(t)\):
\begin{equation}
    \text{Complexity} = \frac{\text{Mobility}(y')}{\text{Mobility}(y)} = \frac{\sigma_{y''} / \sigma_{y'}}{\sigma_{y'} / \sigma_{y}}
\end{equation}
where \(\sigma_{y''}\) is the standard deviation of the change in slope. A pure sine wave gives a Complexity of 1. Complexity rises as the shape of \(y(t)\) departs from a sine wave \cite{hjorth_eeg_1970}.

\subsection{Peak Frequency and Macro Frequency}
\label{sec:method_peak_frequency}
The present thesis defines Peak Frequency and Macro Frequency without prior validation (Section~\ref{sec:lit_peak_frequency}). Both are purely experimental mathematical constructs created by the author after noticing repeated hill patterns in the EEG data, and therefore require further validation beyond the present thesis. Peak Frequency and Macro Frequency count per second how often \(y(t)\), the band strength, peaks. Peak Frequency counts every bump. Macro Frequency counts only the larger hills. The counts need not equal the frequency of the waves within the band (Section~\ref{sec:lit_peak_frequency}).

\paragraph{Peak Frequency.} A peak is a sample of \(y(t)\) higher than both neighbouring samples. \(P\) is the set of all peaks in the window. Peak Frequency divides the number of peaks by the window duration:
\begin{equation}
    f_{peak} = \frac{|P|}{T}
\end{equation}
The z-scoring shifts and scales the envelope, and leaves the number of peaks unchanged.

\paragraph{Macro Frequency.} Added noise raises the number of local maxima and minima of a signal \cite{niknazar_new_2018}. Peak Frequency may therefore also count small bumps from noise. Macro Frequency counts the larger hills in three steps:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Threshold.} The threshold \(h_{peak}\) is the mean height of all peaks in the window:
    \begin{equation}
        h_{peak} = \frac{1}{|P|} \sum_{p \in P} y(p)
    \end{equation}
    \item \textbf{Mark.} Every sample of \(y(t)\) at or above \(h_{peak}\) becomes 1. Every other sample becomes 0. The result is a sequence of ones and zeros, \(B(t)\).
    \item \textbf{Bridge.} A hill can hold a short dip below the threshold. \(\bar{G}\) is the mean length of all runs of zeros in \(B(t)\). Every run of zeros between two ones that is no longer than \(\bar{G}\) becomes ones. The result is \(B_{smooth}(t)\).
\end{enumerate}
Each run of ones in \(B_{smooth}(t)\) is one hill. Macro Frequency divides the number of hills by the window duration:
\begin{equation}
    f_{macro} = \frac{\text{number of hills in } B_{smooth}}{T}
\end{equation}
A window without any peak gets a Peak Frequency and a Macro Frequency of 0.

\subsection{Raw Features}
The raw features describe the raw signal, the signal before the split into bands (Section~\ref{sec:method_baseline}). A split into bands can hide activity that spreads over all frequencies: band ratios, such as the ratio of theta to beta, can reflect such spread-out activity rather than the named bands \cite{donoghue_electrophysiological_2020}. Three raw features are computed at each electrode:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Mean:} the level of the raw signal within the window.
    \item \textbf{Standard deviation:} the spread of the raw signal within the window.
    \item \textbf{Slope:} the slope of a straight line fitted to the raw signal of the window, per second. The slope shows the drift of the raw signal within the window.
\end{itemize}

