\subsection{Feature Separation Analysis}
\label{sec:method_feature_separation_cohen_d}
Cohen's \(d\) (Section~\ref{sec:method_class_separation_metric}, Equation~\ref{eq:cohens_d}) ranks the 236 features by how far the \texttt{STAY} windows and the \texttt{SKIP} windows lie apart (executed via \url{https://github.com/coffeeandcoffee/eeg_tiktok_pipeline/blob/main/data_analysis/viz17_feature_ranking.py}). The EI gets a \(|d|\) the same way, as the reference line in the figures of Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}. Cohen's \(d\) carries no unit \cite{cohen_statistical_1988}. Features in different units can therefore share one ranking, for example TP10 raw standard deviation in microvolts and AF8 delta peak frequency in peaks per second. \(|d|\) serves only to pick candidate features. No statistical test is run on \(|d|\); the tests of Section~\ref{sec:method_metrics_and_significance} compare models instead.

Cohen's \(d\) is reported at two levels:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Single features:} the exact \(|d|\) of one named feature, such as TP10 raw standard deviation (Table~\ref{tab:cohend_top_features}).
    \item \textbf{Categories:} the mean \(|d|\) over all features of one band, one electrode or one statistic, and over all features of one band at one electrode.
\end{itemize}
The categories differ in size. A band holds eight statistics per electrode, and the raw signal holds three (Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_engineering}). A comparison between two category means is therefore rough. The spread within a category shows differences between features, rather than the uncertainty of one value. The category figures therefore show no confidence intervals. The one exception is the figure of each band at each electrode. The band-by-electrode figure shows bootstrapped 95\% confidence intervals over the eight features, or three raw features, behind each point, so the intervals show the same spread between features.

Five picks lead to the models of Section~\ref{sec:method_baselines_and_logistic_regression}: the two features with the largest \(|d|\) overall, and the feature with the largest \(|d|\) within the strongest band, within the strongest electrode and within the strongest statistic. Strongest means the largest mean \(|d|\) among the bands, among the electrodes or among the statistics. The five picks name three distinct features, the \emph{top features}: TP10 raw standard deviation, AF7 high-gamma mean and AF8 delta peak frequency (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}).

\subsection{Logistic Regression Modeling and Baselines}
\label{sec:method_baselines_and_logistic_regression}
Three kinds of model predict \texttt{STAY} or \texttt{SKIP} for the same test windows (executed via \url{https://github.com/coffeeandcoffee/eeg_tiktok_pipeline/blob/main/data_analysis/step07_train_intra.py} and \url{https://github.com/coffeeandcoffee/eeg_tiktok_pipeline/blob/main/data_analysis/step16_parallel_universes.py}):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Coin-flip baseline.} A classifier guesses \texttt{STAY} or \texttt{SKIP} for every window at random, with equal chance (\texttt{DummyClassifier(strategy='uniform')} of scikit-learn). The coin-flip baseline ignores the EEG and measures the chance level directly (Section~\ref{sec:method_metrics_and_significance}). Random variation moves single values of the coin-flip baseline away from 50\%, for example to 55.0\% for intra-subject \texttt{STAY} recall (Table~\ref{tab:metrics_1_intra}).
    \item \textbf{EI model.} A logistic regression receives the EI of each window as the only input.
    \item \textbf{Top-feature models.} A logistic regression receives one top feature of each window as the only input. Each of the three top features gets a model of its own.
\end{enumerate}
Like the EI, every input is one number per window. A difference between a top-feature model and the EI model therefore reflects the input alone. Before training, the pipeline scales each input to a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1 over the training windows, and applies the same scaling to the test windows (\texttt{StandardScaler} of scikit-learn). The logistic regression runs with \texttt{class\_weight='balanced'} (\texttt{LogisticRegression} of scikit-learn). The training windows are already balanced, so the setting leaves the two classes with equal weight.

\subsection{Metrics and Significance Testing}
\label{sec:method_metrics_and_significance}
Each class takes a turn as the positive class. With \texttt{SKIP} as the positive class, a true positive (TP) is a \texttt{SKIP} window predicted as \texttt{SKIP}, a true negative (TN) a \texttt{STAY} window predicted as \texttt{STAY}, a false positive (FP) a \texttt{STAY} window predicted as \texttt{SKIP}, and a false negative (FN) a \texttt{SKIP} window predicted as \texttt{STAY}. With \texttt{STAY} as the positive class, the two classes swap roles. A correct \texttt{SKIP} prediction finds a window from 5~s to 2~s before a keypress (Section~\ref{sec:op_labeling_params}).

\[ \text{Precision} = \frac{TP}{TP + FP} \qquad \text{Recall} = \frac{TP}{TP + FN} \qquad F_1 = 2 \cdot \frac{\text{Precision} \cdot \text{Recall}}{\text{Precision} + \text{Recall}} \]

Precision asks: of all windows the model calls \texttt{SKIP}, how many are \texttt{SKIP}? Recall asks: of all \texttt{SKIP} windows, how many does the model find? The F\(_1\)-score combines precision and recall and is the primary metric. A model that calls nearly every window \texttt{STAY} reaches a high \texttt{STAY} recall, yet a low \texttt{SKIP} F\(_1\)-score. Reporting the F\(_1\)-score for each class therefore reveals such one-sided models. On balanced test windows, random guessing gives a precision, a recall and an F\(_1\)-score of about 50\% for each class.

For each participant, the pipeline adds up TP, TN, FP and FN over all five folds and all five seeds, and computes each metric once from the sums. In the inter-subject setting, each participant is tested once. Precision and recall come from the test windows. The F\(_1\)-score comes from the test windows (F\(_1\)-test) and from the training windows (F\(_1\)-train). A high F\(_1\)-train with a low F\(_1\)-test points to overfitting, a fit to the training windows that fails on new windows.

The Wilcoxon signed-rank test compares two models across the 25 participants. Each participant gives one pair of scores, for example the F\(_1\)-test of the EI model and the F\(_1\)-test of the coin-flip baseline. The test ranks the 25 differences by size and asks whether the positive and the negative differences balance out. Demšar recommends the Wilcoxon signed-rank test for comparing two classifiers over several data sets, here the 25 participants, because the test assumes no normal distribution and gives outliers less weight \cite{demsar_statistical_2006}. Demšar also treats the data sets as independent \cite{demsar_statistical_2006}. In the intra-subject setting, the scores of each participant come from the data of that participant alone. In the inter-subject setting, the models for two participants share 23 training participants, so the scores of different participants are not fully independent.

Two comparisons are tested: the EI model against the coin-flip baseline, and each top-feature model against the EI model. Each comparison runs in the intra-subject setting and in the inter-subject setting, for eight metrics: F\(_1\)-train, F\(_1\)-test, precision and recall, each with \texttt{STAY} and with \texttt{SKIP} as the positive class. The test is two-sided at a significance level of 0.05, so the test reacts to a model that is better and to a model that is worse. A model counts as better only when \(p < 0.05\) and the mean score of the model over the participants lies above the mean score of the baseline. The result tables mark the better values in green.

The eight result tables hold 64 tests. The \(p\)-values are not corrected for multiple comparisons. At a significance level of 0.05, about 3 of 64 tests would reach \(p < 0.05\) by chance, even without any real difference between the models. A single significant result therefore serves as exploratory screening. A correction for multiple comparisons is left to future research (Section~\ref{sec:future_research}).


\chapter{Results and Discussion}
\label{chap:results}

The present chapter reports the six contributions of the present thesis (Section~\ref{sec:contributions}), one section each:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{The experiment} (Section~\ref{sec:results_exp_learnings}; Methods Sections~\ref{sec:method_participants}--\ref{sec:method_experimental_protocol}). Twenty-five participants browsed TikTok while a consumer-grade headband recorded the EEG. The experiment ran in a partly controlled setting that encouraged naturalistic use. The dataset and the experimental protocol form the basis of every later analysis.

\item \textbf{The reusable pipeline} (Section~\ref{sec:results_pipeline}; Methods Sections~\ref{sec:method_universal_recording_pipeline}--\ref{sec:method_result_synthesis_pipeline}). The full pipeline runs from recording to results on a laptop, with public code. Other studies may reuse the full pipeline.

\item \textbf{Swipe bursts} (Section~\ref{sec:results_burst_analysis}; Methods Section~\ref{sec:method_behavioral_burst_analysis}). Swipes often cluster into bursts. The burst analysis answers none of the three research questions, and is therefore a secondary result.

\item \textbf{Engagement Index performance} (Section~\ref{sec:results_engagement_index}; Methods Sections~\ref{sec:method_feature_engineering}--\ref{sec:method_result_synthesis_pipeline}). Cohen's \(d\) compares how well the EI and the 236 features separate \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP} (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}). Logistic regression models then test the EI against the coin-flip baseline, and the three top features against the EI (Sections~\ref{sec:results_model_performance} and~\ref{sec:results_alternative_index}).

\item \textbf{Discussion} (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}). The Discussion interprets the results for neuroscience, psychology, behaviour and application.

\item \textbf{A proposed definition of engagement} (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated}). Informed by the results, Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated} proposes a general definition of engagement, four reporting requirements, a hypothesis for field measurement, and two findings that would show the proposed definition wrong. The present data do not test the proposed definition.
\end{enumerate}

Section~\ref{sec:results_engagement_index} answers the first two research questions, separability and benchmark comparison (Section~\ref{sec:intro_research_questions}). Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated} answers the third research question, definition.

