\section{Lessons from Running the Experiment}
\label{sec:results_exp_learnings}

The experiment worked in a partly controlled setting (Section~\ref{sec:method_experimental_protocol}). The setting still encouraged naturalistic use: self-paced viewing of an algorithmic feed, with a swipe possible at any moment (Section~\ref{sec:adjacent_studies}). No session had a major interruption. Recruitment through email and university mailing lists yielded 28 participants. Most participants were academically oriented (Section~\ref{sec:method_participants}). The author observed that many participants showed interest in the research question.

Guided Access, an iPhone setting, helped in both the baseline phase and the browsing phase. Guided Access kept the iPhone in the open app. Guided Access also ended each phase after a set time: 2 minutes for the baseline phase, and 25 minutes for the browsing phase.

The briefing, meaning the instructions the author gave participants during the session, was hard to keep the same across sessions only from mental notes. After the first few sessions they were written down to standardize the process even better. The briefing explained the scrolling task, the free TikTok browsing of the browsing phase. The briefing covered the ``A'' keypress at each swipe, the start and the end of the scrolling task, and what to do when the recording paused due to a Bluetooth dropout. After a Bluetooth dropout, participants were asked to simply keep scrolling. The author would pause or end the experiment only if a participant felt unwell, which did not happen. The wording of the briefing varied between sessions. The effect of the varying wording on the data was not measured. Future replications would benefit from a fixed briefing script and a fixed session flow.

\section{The Reusable Pipeline}
\label{sec:results_pipeline}

The full pipeline is all code of the present thesis, from recording to results: the reproducible recording pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_recording_pipeline}), the reproducible preprocessing pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_preprocessing_pipeline}), feature engineering (Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_engineering}) and the result synthesis pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:method_result_synthesis_pipeline}). After several months of development, the full pipeline ran from recording to results.

The main technical problem was the Bluetooth dropout. The EEG during each Bluetooth dropout was lost (Section~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}). The reproducible recording pipeline handled each Bluetooth dropout: the reproducible recording pipeline reconnected and continued in a new recording file. The reproducible preprocessing pipeline processed each recording file on its own, so the EEG before and after each Bluetooth dropout stayed usable for the analysis (Section~\ref{sec:method_sync}). No Bluetooth dropout caused a major session failure. The loss of EEG during each Bluetooth dropout leaves room for improvement.

During development, much of the code became obsolete. The code shrank from 37,189 to 11,311 lines: 69.6\% of the lines were temporary, diagnostic, or replaced by better versions. Future projects could archive or delete unused scripts at regular steps. Less code is easier to read, maintain, reuse and extend.

The full pipeline needs a Muse headband, a laptop and open-source Python libraries. The full pipeline uses no data from TikTok: keypresses mark the swipes (Section~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}). The full pipeline is designed for reuse. The ``A'' key can mark any action of interest, and one configuration file sets the frequency bands, the electrodes and the analysis steps. The author tested the reuse of the full pipeline successfully. The code is public at \url{https://github.com/coffeeandcoffee/eeg_tiktok_pipeline}, so each step can be rerun. Other studies that record consumer-grade EEG and mark actions with keypresses may reuse the full pipeline.

\section{Behavioural Swipe-Burst Phenomenon} \label{sec:results_burst_analysis}

A burst is several rapid swipes in a row (Section~\ref{sec:method_behavioral_burst_analysis}). Each swipe gets a $\pm$3.0~s burst window. Swipes less than 6.0~s apart have overlapping burst windows and merge into one burst block. The \emph{block size} is the number of swipes in one burst block. A burst block of size 1 is a single swipe with no other swipe within 6.0~s before or after the swipe.

The burst analysis counts ``A'' keypresses, not swipes. Every count below therefore relies on each participant following the instructions: one keypress at every swipe to the next video, and no other action in the app (Section~\ref{sec:method_experimental_protocol}). Participants reported keypress errors for about 1\% of keypresses (Section~\ref{sec:method_participants}).

Of all 3{,}338 swipes, 1{,}344 (40.3\%) stand alone in a burst block of size 1. The other 59.7\% of swipes lie in bursts. Counted by burst block instead of by swipe, 1{,}344 of 1{,}994 burst blocks (67.4\%) have size 1. Burst blocks of size 1 are therefore the most common burst blocks, and the largest burst block holds 23 swipes (Figure~\ref{fig:bursts_all_3s}).

Participants differ widely (Figure~\ref{fig:bursts_indiv_3s}). Per participant, the share of burst blocks of size 1 runs from 32.5\% (P17) to 100\% (P5), with a mean of 69.1\% and a standard deviation of 16.2 percentage points. The present thesis calls participants with many large burst blocks \emph{fast scrollers}, for example P17. The present thesis calls participants with mostly burst blocks of size 1 \emph{slow scrollers}, for example P15. The two labels name the two ends of one range, not two separate groups. Each participant was recorded in one 25-minute session only, so the present data cannot show whether a participant scrolls the same way on another day.

The burst window changes every share above. With a $\pm$5.0~s burst window, swipes less than 10.0~s apart merge (Figure~\ref{fig:bursts_all_5s}). Only 17.0\% of swipes then stand alone, and 48.9\% of burst blocks have size 1. Per participant, the share of burst blocks of size 1 then has a mean of 46.9\% and a standard deviation of 20.8 percentage points. The largest burst block then holds 43 swipes. The author chose the $\pm$3.0~s burst window without a source (Section~\ref{sec:method_behavioral_burst_analysis}), so every share in the present section depends on an unvalidated choice.

