\subsection{EI Model versus Coin-Flip Baseline}
\label{sec:results_model_performance}

The EI model and the coin-flip baseline predict \texttt{STAY} or \texttt{SKIP} for the same balanced test windows (Sections~\ref{sec:method_baselines_and_logistic_regression} and~\ref{sec:method_metrics_and_significance}). The F\(_1\)-score is the primary metric. A green value in the tables is significantly higher than the baseline (\(p < 0.05\)). The \(p\)-values are uncorrected across 64 tests, so a green value is an exploratory finding (Section~\ref{sec:method_metrics_and_significance}).

In the intra-subject setting (Table~\ref{tab:metrics_1_intra}), the EI model never beats the coin-flip baseline on the test windows. On four test metrics, the EI model scores significantly lower than the coin-flip baseline: F\(_1\)-test, precision and recall for \texttt{STAY}, and precision for \texttt{SKIP}. Every value of both models lies between 45\% and 55\%.

In the inter-subject setting (Table~\ref{tab:metrics_1_inter}), the EI model scores significantly higher than the coin-flip baseline on two \texttt{STAY} test metrics: F\(_1\)-test (62.5\%) and recall (89.2\%). The EI model scores significantly lower on all four \texttt{SKIP} metrics, down to 9.6\% \texttt{SKIP} recall. \texttt{STAY} precision stays at 49.4\%, the level of chance. The EI model calls about 90\% of all windows \texttt{STAY}, whether a window is \texttt{STAY} or \texttt{SKIP}: the EI model is one-sided (Section~\ref{sec:method_metrics_and_significance}). The high \texttt{STAY} recall reflects the one-sided calling and does not show that the EI model tells \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP}. On balanced test windows, a model that calls every window \texttt{STAY} would reach a \texttt{STAY} F\(_1\)-score of about 67\%, above the 62.5\% of the EI model.

