\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:results_interpretation}

The present section asks what the results mean. Three results carry the discussion. First, every band, every electrode and the raw signal separated \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP} better than the EI, yet every \(|d|\) stayed below 0.2 (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}). Second, no model predicted \texttt{STAY} or \texttt{SKIP} clearly better than chance, within or across participants (Sections~\ref{sec:results_model_performance} and~\ref{sec:results_alternative_index}). Third, swipes often came in bursts (Section~\ref{sec:results_burst_analysis}). The results come from one dataset of 25 participants, and the origin of every feature remains open. Every reading below is therefore a possibility for future tests.

The small \(|d|\) explains why prediction stayed near chance. A \(|d|\) below 0.2 means that the \texttt{STAY} windows and the \texttt{SKIP} windows overlap almost completely. Body height gives an everyday example. Imagine two football teams. The players of team~A are on average about 1.5~cm taller than the players of team~B. The two averages differ, yet the heights of the two teams overlap almost completely: many players of team~B are taller than many players of team~A. Someone who sees only the height of one player can therefore barely guess the team of that player. The windows of the present thesis behave the same way. TP10 raw standard deviation differs on average between \texttt{STAY} windows and \texttt{SKIP} windows (\(|d| = 0.196\)), yet a model that sees one window can barely guess the label of that window.

\subsection{Neuroscience}

\textbf{Why the EI separated so little.} The EI separated \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP} least of all tested signals (\(|d| = 0.022\)). Across participants, the EI model was one-sided and called about 90\% of all windows \texttt{STAY} (Section~\ref{sec:results_model_performance}). Within participants, the EI model scored near chance. The weak EI result fits one earlier result: in a controlled learning task, Apicella et al.\ found the accuracy of the EI ``not optimal'', and note that the EI is mainly used in non-predictive applications \cite{apicella_eeg-based_2022}. Three explanations remain open. First, the present thesis tested an adapted EI. The adapted EI uses the variance of the z-scored envelope, the four Muse sites and beta at 13--30~Hz (Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_engineering}). Pope et al.\ used band power at the electrode sites Cz, Pz, P3 and P4, with beta at 13--22~Hz \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}. Part of the weak result may therefore belong to the adaptation. Second, Pope et al.\ chose the EI for a different job. In the task of Pope et al., a computer switched a tracking task between manual and automated operation within seconds, so every change in task demand came from outside the participant \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}. A swipe comes from inside the participant, without any cue. Third, the EI averages each band over all four sites. Alpha often changes locally: at one moment, alpha can rise at one site and fall at another \cite{pfurtscheller_event-related_1996}. An average over two forehead sites and two sites behind the ears may blur such local changes.

\textbf{What the EI may track.} The EI has separated whole tasks well. Nuamah et al.\ recognised five mental tasks from the EI in 10-second trials, with 88.67\% accuracy where guessing gives 20\% (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definitions}) \cite{nuamah_electroencephalography_2017}. Ovi et al.\ averaged the EI over each whole task of each participant, and the EI separated a cognitive task from an affective task with a large effect (\(d = -1.03\)) \cite{ovi_frequency_2026}. In the present thesis, the EI separated 1-second windows before a swipe from sustained viewing only slightly. Taken together, the EI may track which kind of task a person performs over many seconds, rather than the moment before a self-paced action. Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated} returns to the role of the time interval.

\textbf{Where the three top features may come from.} Each top feature may reflect brain activity, muscle activity, powerline-noise, or the person being recorded. The present thesis cannot tell these sources apart (Section~\ref{sec:lit_emg}). EMG and EEG share frequencies, like the two singers of Section~\ref{sec:lit_emg}. Most scalp EEG above 20~Hz is EMG, even in a relaxed person, and the share of EMG is largest at the lower edge of the scalp \cite{whitham_scalp_2007}. Muscle activity also changes with mental effort \cite{mcmenamin_electromyogenic_2011}. A change in muscle tension before a swipe could therefore separate \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP} just as a change in brain activity could.

\emph{TP10 raw standard deviation} (\(|d| = 0.196\)) measures how much the raw signal behind the right ear spreads within one window. TP10 sits next to the small muscles around the ear and near the jaw \cite{mcmenamin_electromyogenic_2011}. The raw signal keeps the 50~Hz powerline-noise, because no notch filter was applied (Section~\ref{sec:lit_notch_high_gamma}). The raw signal is also not z-scored (Section~\ref{sec:method_baseline}). Differences in signal strength between participants, and between the two headband units (Section~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}), therefore stay in TP10 raw standard deviation. In one EEG study, a broadband signal identified individual people better than any single band \cite{ovi_frequency_2026}. TP10 raw standard deviation may therefore partly reflect which participant, and which headband, is recorded.

\emph{AF7 high-gamma mean} (\(|d| = 0.168\)) measures the strength of 40--60~Hz on the left forehead. A brain reading exists: attention to a stimulus goes with stronger and faster gamma synchronisation \cite{fries_rhythms_2015}. The evidence for the brain reading comes mainly from recordings inside the visual cortex of monkeys \cite{fries_rhythms_2015}, far from a forehead electrode. Two other sources are at least as plausible. AF7 lies near the frontalis, the forehead muscle (Section~\ref{sec:lit_emg}), and Pope et al.\ themselves treated power at 42--100~Hz as EMG \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}. The 50~Hz powerline-noise also lies in the middle of high gamma (Section~\ref{sec:lit_notch_high_gamma}). McMenamin et al.\ advise extreme caution for beta and gamma findings near scalp muscles, and ask at minimum for a scalp map of where each effect lies \cite{mcmenamin_electromyogenic_2011}. Four electrodes cannot give such a map.

\emph{AF8 delta peak frequency} (\(|d| = 0.140\)) counts how often per second the strength of the delta band peaks on the right forehead (Section~\ref{sec:method_peak_frequency}). A brain reading exists here too. Reviews link delta to attention, decision making and salience detection \cite{guntekin_review_2016, knyazev_eeg_2012}, and in one EEG study, delta separated two tasks best of all bands \cite{ovi_frequency_2026}. TikTok, the app used by the participants of the present thesis, has a Chinese version called Douyin. In adolescents browsing Douyin, the change in delta during browsing predicted an addiction score \cite{xu_neural_2024}. The cited works study mainly the strength of delta, and none labels the seconds before a self-paced swipe (Section~\ref{sec:lit_delta}). The link to AF8 delta peak frequency is therefore loose. Low frequencies are not free of muscle either: near a tensed muscle, EMG reaches every band, delta included \cite{goncharova_emg_2003}. AF8 also sits above the right eye, where the artifact check looks for blinks (Section~\ref{sec:method_artifact_analysis}). Whether blinks change AF8 delta peak frequency was not tested.

\textbf{Left and right.} AF7 and TP10 separated the classes more than AF8 and TP9 (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}). The pattern may point to a difference between the two sides of the brain. Simpler explanations exist as well. Two headband units were used. The participant swiped with one hand and pressed the ``A'' key with the other hand (Section~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}), and the present thesis recorded neither handedness nor the hand holding the phone. The left--right pattern therefore remains a question for future research.

\subsection{Psychology}

The present data suggest that leaving a video may differ from a low level of engagement. Pooled over all participants, the seconds before a swipe differed slightly from sustained viewing in many features (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}). The EI, a composite of three bands, captured the difference least of all. The working definition treats the swipe as the latest observable end of engagement (Section~\ref{sec:intro_engagement_definition}), and the small differences are compatible with a short event before that end. The present data cannot show what the event is: a decision, a loss of interest, the preparation of the swipe, or muscle tension.

The prediction results add no evidence for such an event. Across participants, the top-feature models scored higher than the EI model on all four \texttt{SKIP} metrics (Section~\ref{sec:results_alternative_index}). Yet each top-feature model called \texttt{SKIP} about as often for \texttt{STAY} windows as for \texttt{SKIP} windows. The higher \texttt{SKIP} scores therefore reflect less one-sided calling than the EI model. Within participants, every model stayed near chance (Sections~\ref{sec:results_model_performance} and~\ref{sec:results_alternative_index}). The only sign of a difference before the swipe remains the small pooled \(|d|\).

Two kinds of swipe may mix in the \texttt{SKIP} class. Some swipes may follow a deliberate judgement of the video. The forehead sites lie over the prefrontal cortex, which is held to be particularly important for top-down control, the guiding of thought and action by internal goals \cite{miller_integrative_2001}. Other swipes may happen on autopilot. Users of a social media feed describe getting ``lost'' in the feed \cite{baughan_i_2022}, and Miller and Cohen note that the prefrontal cortex is not critical for simple, automatic behaviours \cite{miller_integrative_2001}. A mix of the two kinds of swipe would blur any single signal. The present thesis did not record which kind of swipe occurred. The burst analysis gives the closest behavioural hint (Section~\ref{sec:results_burst_analysis}): a swipe after long viewing may more often follow a deliberate judgement, and a swipe inside a burst may more often happen on autopilot. The hint remains untested.

Participants differed widely. Burst tendency varied strongly between participants (Section~\ref{sec:results_burst_analysis}), and no model transferred to new participants better than chance. In one fMRI study, prefrontal and other higher-order areas did not synchronise across five people watching the same film, which Hasson et al.\ link to individual variations \cite{hasson_intersubject_2004}. Cross-subject EEG classification is a recognised challenge even in controlled laboratory tasks \cite{apicella_eeg-based_2022, bellos_methods_2025}. EMG also differs strongly between people \cite{mcmenamin_electromyogenic_2011}. One difference to Hasson et al.\ matters: the five people of Hasson et al.\ watched the same film, whereas each participant of the present thesis saw a different feed. Part of the weak transfer may therefore come from different videos as well as from different brains. Within participants, the models also stayed near chance, so the present data cannot separate a person-specific signal from a missing signal.

\subsection{Behaviour}

Swipes often came in bursts: 59.7\% of swipes lay in bursts under the \(\pm\)3.0~s burst window, and 83.0\% under a \(\pm\)5.0~s burst window (Section~\ref{sec:results_burst_analysis}). The swipes of one burst may not be independent decisions. The \texttt{SKIP} class therefore mixes swipes after long viewing with swipes a few seconds after the previous swipe. In bursts, 23\% of all \texttt{SKIP} windows lie within the pre-swipe gap or the post-swipe gap of a neighbouring swipe (Section~\ref{sec:op_stay_skip}). The sensitivity analysis points the same way. The largest \(|d|\) rose as the \texttt{SKIP} windows moved further from the swipe, and so closer to the previous swipe (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}). Rimbert et al.\ attribute a similar gain partly to activity after the previous movement \cite{rimbert_impact_2023}. Part of the separation between \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} may therefore belong to the previous swipe rather than to the coming swipe.

Every share and every \(|d|\) above depends on labeling parameters and a burst window set by the author (Sections~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity} and~\ref{sec:method_behavioral_burst_analysis}). Future behavioural analyses should test whether a pattern holds across several reasonable values.

\subsection{Application}

The present results support no applied system yet. No model predicted a swipe clearly better than chance, and muscle activity, powerline-noise and differences between participants may drive the top features. The applied value lies in three narrower results. The full pipeline records consumer-grade EEG with a time mark for every swipe, runs from recording to results with public code, and uses no data from TikTok (Section~\ref{sec:results_pipeline}). Features with a known meaning, such as the strength of one band at one electrode, can be compared one by one with the EI. And the EI, the standard EEG measure of engagement, separated the seconds before a swipe from sustained viewing least of all tested signals. The recommendations below rest on these three results and on the reviewed literature. Each recommendation states whether the recommendation rests on the present results or on the reviewed literature.

\subsubsection{Recommendations for Policy Makers}

First, \textbf{report more than watch time}. Social media designers are tasked with maximising time on site \cite{baughan_i_2022}. Long viewing can reflect absorbed attention or zoned-out scrolling: in two daily-life studies, time spent in a feed was not significantly associated with dissociation, and more social media use than usual went with fewer flow reports \cite{baughan_i_2022, knierim_flow_2026}. Knierim et al.\ advise platforms to measure the quality of the experience rather than time on the platform alone \cite{knierim_flow_2026}. Transparency reports and audits could therefore require engagement measures checked against recall or behaviour change. The recommendation rests on the reviewed literature. The present EEG results did not measure dissociation, flow or recall.

Second, \textbf{test interface features that interrupt scrolling}. On a custom Twitter app, custom lists and reading-history labels went with lower self-reported dissociation \cite{baughan_i_2022}. The evidence comes from one study of text posts, with daily averages that cannot show cause and effect. Replication on short-form video platforms should come before regulation. The recommendation rests on the reviewed literature.

Third, \textbf{treat engagement optimisation as auditable}. In the present thesis, one researcher with a consumer headband and a laptop measured small average EEG differences before a swipe. Platforms likely hold far richer behavioural data. The present thesis has no access to platform data, so the point is a reasonable extrapolation. Regulators could require platforms to disclose which behavioural target a feed optimises, such as continued viewing, because long viewing can also reflect zoned-out scrolling \cite{baughan_i_2022}.

\subsubsection{Recommendations for Digital Global Public Health Research}

For digital public health research on disease prevention and evidence-based health communication, three extensions follow.

First, \textbf{pre-test health messages}. The full pipeline needs a consumer headband, a laptop and open-source software (Section~\ref{sec:results_pipeline}). Small pre-tests of health messages, such as two versions of a vaccination message, therefore become more affordable for public institutions. EEG studies already compare advertisements \cite{zhang_how_2025-1}. The full pipeline took several months of development (Section~\ref{sec:results_pipeline}), so the full pipeline lowers the barrier to entry but remains a research tool. A pre-test would also need a validated signal, which the present thesis does not yet provide. The recommendation rests on the present results.

Second, \textbf{report when viewers leave a health message}. Studies of health messages on TikTok mostly count views, likes and comments \cite{mccashin_using_2023, chen_factors_2021}. In one content analysis, institutional accounts received fewer likes, comments and shares than personal creators, in a comparison without a statistical test that rests on two public-health videos \cite{mccashin_using_2023}. Campaigns could also report churn, the share of viewers who leave during each quarter of a video, as Salminen et al.\ did for advertisements \cite{salminen_engagement_2024}. Churn comes from TikTok analytics and needs no EEG. The present thesis adds the reason to look at the moment of leaving: in the working definition, the swipe is the latest observable end of engagement (Section~\ref{sec:intro_engagement_definition}).

Third, \textbf{expect viewers to differ}. Precision public health tailors prevention to groups with different needs. In the present thesis, participants differed widely in burst tendency (Section~\ref{sec:results_burst_analysis}). One session per participant cannot show whether burst tendency stays stable across days. If burst tendency proves stable, health messages could be tailored to groups of viewers, and one message design for all viewers would reach some viewers poorly. The recommendation rests on the present results and remains a hypothesis.

\subsubsection{Recommendations for Health Message Design}

(i) \textbf{Win interest early.} Most viewers left TikTok advertisements within the first quarter of the video \cite{salminen_engagement_2024}. The present thesis placed the \texttt{SKIP} windows 5 to 2~s before each swipe by argument (Section~\ref{sec:op_labeling_params}), and the seconds before a swipe differed only slightly from sustained viewing. Where in the opening seconds of a video such a difference is strongest, and which content features relate to the difference, remains for future research. The recommendation rests mainly on the reviewed literature.

(ii) \textbf{Judge messages by recall and behaviour change.} A message watched to the end while zoned out may be remembered worse than a shorter message that holds attention \cite{baughan_i_2022, debettencourt_sustained_2021}. Evaluation should therefore weight recall and behaviour change above the share of viewers who watch to the end. The recommendation rests on the reviewed literature.

(iii) \textbf{Expect bursts.} Swipes cluster into bursts, and burst tendency varies sharply between participants (Section~\ref{sec:results_burst_analysis}). By the definition of a burst, a video inside a burst is viewed for less than 6~s. Whether a message inside a burst is remembered worse was not tested. Burst-aware timing of messages therefore remains a question for future research. Until then, campaigns may lower the risk that a single message falls into a burst by spreading several short messages over time, each with a different opening hook. The recommendation rests on the present results, and the spreading of messages is untested.

\subsubsection{Societal Scope: An Explicitly Speculative Outlook}

The following paragraph changes register. The paragraph states a hypothesis, not a result of the present thesis. Knierim et al.\ found that more social media use than usual went with fewer flow experiences, and name displacement as one possible explanation: scrolling may fill time that active pursuits could otherwise use \cite{knierim_flow_2026}. Knierim et al.\ tie flow to such active pursuits, among them learning, creating and conversing \cite{knierim_flow_2026}. Displacement may therefore also reduce time and energy for other people, including the in-person contact through which relationships form. The present thesis did not test displacement, and can neither support the hypothesis nor show the hypothesis to be wrong. Knierim et al.\ also note that social media can serve rest \cite{knierim_flow_2026}. If displacement exists, measures of the moment a viewer leaves, such as the swipe studied here, could help evaluate interventions against displacement, such as interface features that interrupt scrolling \cite{baughan_i_2022}.

Finally, a dual-use note. A marker that helps health communicators hold attention could equally help commercial or political actors. The present thesis therefore argues for open research under ethics oversight (Section~\ref{sec:method_participants}), paired with the third recommendation for policy makers.

\section{Implication: Engagement Definition}
\label{sec:engagement_updated}

The sections above concerned one behaviour on one platform: the swipe. The present section steps outside the swipe. The present section proposes a general definition of engagement, informed by the results of the present thesis and by the definitions reviewed in Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definitions}. This definition is by no means complete nor validated. Its mere purpose is to start a discussion about a general definition of engagement crossing multiple academic fields which, as far as the author's knowledge allows to see, seems under-discussed. The intended continuation of this thought is, that later work in other settings can use, test and most importantly correct the proposed definition. The proposed definition is a proposal about the construct, and the present data do not test the proposed definition. Section~\ref{sec:engagement_falsification} states starting points of what would show the proposed definition to be wrong.

\subsection{Four Findings}
\label{sec:engagement_constraints}

Four findings motivate a more general definition of Engagement.

\paragraph{Staying is no proof of engagement.}
Although longer interaction is often equated with engagement, the evidence does not support this. Long viewing can reflect either absorbed attention or zoned-out scrolling, and sustained attention fluctuates while a person remains in a task (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_short_form}). The present thesis did not measure attention during \texttt{STAY} viewing, so this conclusion rests on the reviewed literature (Section~\ref{sec:op_stay_skip}). The finding therefore argues against defining engagement solely in terms of time spent.

\paragraph{The swipe is a datable end.}
A keypress timestamps every swipe (Section~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}). Although EEG before a swipe differed only slightly from sustained viewing, with \(|d|\) at most 0.196 (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}), the datable end arises from the behaviour itself: the swipe. Some swipes may occur on autopilot, after engagement has already faded (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}), but the swipe still marks the latest possible end of engagement, if not the exact end. This finding argues against a definition based merely on a declining level of engagement and instead proposes a precise moment of definite disengagement.

\paragraph{No single signal defines engagement.}
Pooled across participants, many single features separated \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP} better than the EI (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}). Yet when used as the sole input to a model, the same features performed near chance both within and across participants (Section~\ref{sec:results_alternative_index}). The present data cannot distinguish a person-specific signal from a missing signal (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}). The finding therefore argues against definitions that rely on a narrow signal, such as AF7 high-gamma mean, the EI ratio, likes, or interaction duration, and proposes more holistic metrics, with outcome-oriented behaviour-change metrics being the most desirable.

\paragraph{A number means little without an interval.}
The largest \(|d|\) increased with longer pre-swipe gaps, SKIP periods, and window lengths (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}). The EI separated whole tasks over many seconds in other studies, yet separated 1-second windows before a swipe only slightly (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}). Measured over different intervals, the same type of signal yields different values. This finding therefore argues against reporting engagement without specifying the measurement interval, reinforcing a holistic approach that considers the environment in which the interval and measurement are embedded.

\subsection{The Proposed Definition}
\label{sec:engagement_definition}

The proposed definition inherits the limits of the four findings. The effects are small, come from one dataset of 25 demographically narrow participants, and may partly reflect muscle activity or powerline-noise (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}). The proposed definition is therefore a first version, open to correction. The proposed definition keeps the working definition of Section~\ref{sec:intro_engagement_definition} and replaces the four domain-bound parts: the viewer becomes a person, the content on screen becomes an object, the next video becomes the available alternatives, and the swipe becomes an observable exit.

\begin{center}
\fbox{%
\begin{minipage}{0.92\textwidth}
\vspace{0.5em}
\textbf{Proposed definition (Engagement).} \textit{Engagement is a person's attention to a particular object, held against available alternatives, and ending at the latest at an observable exit.}
\vspace{0.5em}
\end{minipage}}
\end{center}

\textbf{\textit{Attention}} rules out dissociated viewing, in which a person scrolls a feed without really paying attention to the scrolling \cite{baughan_i_2022}. Time spent, used as the only metric, counts the dissociated viewer and the attentive viewer the same way (Staying is no proof of engagement).

\textbf{\textit{To a particular object}} requires engagement to be engagement with something, such as a message, a task, a person or a piece of work. The marketing literature likewise names the object of engagement, such as a brand or a community \cite{trunfio_conceptualising_2021}. ``An engaged user'' without an object is not yet a measurable claim.

\textbf{\textit{Held against available alternatives}} means that the person could turn to something else at any moment. In the infinite feed, the next video is always one thumb movement away.

\textbf{\textit{Ending at the latest at an observable exit}} gives engagement an end that can be dated. The exit is the latest possible end. Engagement may fade earlier without any visible sign, as in dissociated viewing or in a swipe on autopilot. The swipe is a datable end. O'Brien and Toms likewise describe disengagement as ceasing to be engaged, and also as stopping use \cite{obrien_what_2008}.

\paragraph{What the proposed definition inherits.} From O'Brien and Toms: engagement is a process of four stages, from the point of engagement through sustained engagement and disengagement to re-engagement \cite{obrien_what_2008}.

\paragraph{Where the proposed definition departs.} Nuamah et al., citing earlier work, define task engagement as ``effortful striving towards task goals'' \cite{nuamah_electroencephalography_2017}. The computer-science and HCI literatures often treat engagement as flow \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}, a state of deep, effortless absorption \cite{knierim_flow_2026}. The proposed definition deliberately leaves out the word \emph{effortful}, so that flow can count as engagement. The word \emph{attention} already keeps dissociated viewing out, so the proposed definition does not become too narrow.

\paragraph{What the proposed definition drops.} Duration becomes an indicator. Duration covers two things here: the period over which a person uses an intervention, part of the definition of Perski et al.\ \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}, and the watch time of one video. Counts of use, the most common measure in social-media engagement research \cite{trunfio_conceptualising_2021}, become indicators as well. Every measuring instrument drops out: no index, scale, rating, formula or telemetry (No single signal defines engagement). An EEG feature, a self-report item, a linguistic marker and a behavioural trace can therefore all serve as indicators. None of the indicators is engagement itself.

\paragraph{What the proposed definition adds.} To the author's knowledge, none of the definitions reviewed in Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definitions} ends engagement at an observable exit. The interval enters as a reporting requirement (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_measurement}).

\subsection{Implications for Measurement}
\label{sec:engagement_measurement}

Three implications follow from the proposed definition.

\textbf{Duration is one indicator.} Duration may be reported as one indicator. Duration should not serve as the yardstick against which other measures are validated.

\textbf{The exit is a primary measurement target.} An instrument should be judged by how well the instrument detects the change toward the exit, as well as by how well the instrument scores the middle of viewing. In the present thesis, the adapted EI separated the seconds before a swipe from sustained viewing least of all tested signals (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}). The result concerns the adapted EI, and says nothing yet about engagement itself.

\textbf{Indicators are plural and are checked against each other.} Yardley et al.\ recommend multiple measures of engagement and call for methods to triangulate the measures \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. O'Brien and Toms planned to check self-report against behavioural and physiological measures \cite{obrien_what_2008}.

The finding \emph{A number means little without an interval} and the three implications lead to four reporting requirements. An engagement measurement states:
\begin{itemize}
    \item the \textbf{object} engaged with;
    \item the \textbf{interval} over which engagement is claimed;
    \item the \textbf{indicator} used;
    \item the \textbf{exit criterion}: what counts as an exit, observed directly rather than inferred from time spent alone.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{A Hypothesis for Field Measurement}
\label{sec:engagement_bridge}

EEG is time-precise: EEG records 256 times per second during viewing, unlike a questionnaire filled in afterwards. Yet EEG is hard to use at scale. The EEG pipeline of the present thesis needed a headband, a baseline recording for each participant, a keypress at every swipe, and a quiet room. Yardley et al.\ rate body-route measures as often intrusive, expensive and hard to scale (Section~\ref{sec:health_communication_literature}). Even in a partly controlled setting, no model of the present thesis separated \texttt{SKIP} windows from \texttt{STAY} windows clearly better than chance (Sections~\ref{sec:results_model_performance} and~\ref{sec:results_alternative_index}).

The proposed definition names no measuring instrument, and so leaves room for cheaper instruments. No route measures engagement directly, so no route serves as ground truth. Witnesses offer an analogy. No single witness sees everything. When independent witnesses agree on the time of an event, the account gains credibility. When witnesses disagree, the disagreement adds information. In the analogy, the ask route, the log route and the body route are the witnesses, and the exit is the event.

The present thesis therefore states the following hypothesis. The hypothesis awaits testing in follow-up research.

\begin{quote}
\emph{Where EEG cannot be used at scale, engagement as proposed in Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definition} may be measurable with cheaper field measures: short self-report questionnaires repeated over time (the ask route), and behavioural or linguistic traces in everyday activity and communication (the log route). A field measure gains support when the field measure agrees with indicators from other routes at the exit. The proposed definition implies two checks: the field measure should react at the exit, and the field measure should do more than count time.}
\end{quote}

The hypothesis faces a known obstacle. In the one study Perski et al.\ cite on the question, body-route measures and ask-route measures of engagement agreed only weakly \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}.

The present data cannot test the hypothesis. The present thesis offers only a narrow first step: the keypress dated each exit, and the EEG differed from sustained viewing only slightly before the exit. The present thesis recorded no ask-route measure of engagement, no second setting and no later outcome. In other settings, such as a work setting, EEG is hard to use, and which routes can date an exit there remains open.

\subsection{What Would Show the Proposal Wrong}
\label{sec:engagement_falsification}

Two findings would show the proposed definition to be wrong, or in need of a change, and existing methods can already look for both.

First, an indicator that marks the change toward the exit in new people, without calibration to each person. Keeping every signal out of the proposed definition would then be needless caution, and a definition that names the indicator would serve better. Even the best result of Apicella et al.\ across participants, 72.8\% for two levels of cognitive engagement, first removed the average signal of each participant \cite{apicella_eeg-based_2022}.

Second, dissociated viewing that produces the same recall and the same behaviour change as attentive viewing. The word \emph{attention} would then add nothing.

\subsection{Scope of the Proposal}
\label{sec:engagement_scope}

Nothing in the proposed definition is specific to short-form video. TikTok served as a test case with a convenient property: a frequent, voluntary, time-stamped exit, about 134 swipes per participant in 25 minutes (Section~\ref{sec:method_participants}). The proposed definition is meant also for settings where exits are rarer, slower and harder to see: a learner abandoning a course, a patient dropping out of a DBCI \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}, or a colleague withdrawing from shared work. In such settings, one caution applies: stopping use can also mean that a person no longer needs the intervention \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}.

Under the proposed definition, engagement with a health message in a feed and engagement with shared work in a distributed team can be treated as one construct with different exits. In the feed, the exit is a thumb movement and is logged. In remote work and online collaboration, the exit of engagement is slower and quieter, and may show only in what a person stops contributing to. In remote work, hours logged, messages sent or meetings attended can most easily stand in for engagement unnoticed. The proposed definition asks for an exit criterion in addition.


\chapter{Conclusion and Future Research}
\label{chap:conclusion}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

The present thesis asked three questions. Can EEG show the seconds before a viewer decides to swipe away? Is the EI, \(\beta/(\alpha+\theta)\), the standard EEG measure of engagement, the right instrument for the seconds before a swipe? And which general definition of engagement could serve other fields as a starting point? Twenty-five participants browsed a TikTok ``For You'' feed, reset before each participant, freely and alone for 25 minutes. A four-channel Muse~S headband recorded the EEG, and a keypress marked every swipe. Each 1.0~s window gave 236 features. Cohen's \(d\) ranked the 236 features and the EI. Logistic regression then tested the EI and the three top features, in the intra-subject setting and in the inter-subject setting. The effects are small, and the answers are narrow.

\textbf{Separability.} On average, the seconds before a swipe (\texttt{SKIP}) differed slightly from sustained viewing (\texttt{STAY}). The strongest single feature, TP10 raw standard deviation, reached \(|d| = 0.196\). Every \(|d|\) stayed below 0.2, the value Cohen gives for a small effect, and no statistical test was run on \(|d|\). No model predicted \texttt{STAY} or \texttt{SKIP} clearly better than chance. The data therefore suggest a small average difference. The data do not show that the small average difference can predict a single swipe.

\textbf{Benchmark comparison.} Every band, every electrode and the raw signal separated \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP} better than the EI. The EI reached \(|d| = 0.022\), and TP10 raw standard deviation about nine times as much. In the intra-subject setting, the EI model scored near chance. In the inter-subject setting, the EI model was one-sided: the EI model called about 90\% of all windows \texttt{STAY}. \texttt{STAY} recall reached 89.2\%, \texttt{SKIP} recall only 9.6\%, and \texttt{STAY} precision stayed at 49.4\%, the level of chance. Each top-feature model detected \texttt{SKIP} significantly better than the EI model in the inter-subject setting, on all four \texttt{SKIP} metrics. Yet \texttt{SKIP} F\(_1\)-test reached at most 44.3\%, below the chance level of about 50\%. Each top-feature model called \texttt{SKIP} about as often for \texttt{STAY} windows as for \texttt{SKIP} windows. The top-feature models were therefore less one-sided than the EI model, without separating \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP} better than chance. In the intra-subject setting, TP10 raw standard deviation beat the EI model on no test metric. AF7 high-gamma mean and AF8 delta peak frequency beat the EI model on a few test metrics, among the \texttt{SKIP} metrics only on \texttt{SKIP} precision, and every model stayed near chance. The present thesis has therefore found no better index. In the present data, the EI separated the seconds before a swipe from sustained viewing least of all tested signals.

