\textbf{Parameter sensitivity.} The largest \(|d|\) of any single feature rose with every longer pre-swipe gap, every longer SKIP period and every longer window length. Across the grid of pre-swipe gap and SKIP period, the largest \(|d|\) ran from 0.158 to 0.236. Every \(|d|\) of the present thesis therefore depends on the labeling parameters. The default pre-swipe gap rests on the literature on movement preparation, and the default window length on the literature on window length. To the author's knowledge, no study gives a value for the SKIP period before a self-paced action. The default values were fixed before the sensitivity analysis.

\textbf{Bursts.} Swipes often came in bursts. Under the \(\pm\)3.0~s burst window, 40.3\% of swipes stood alone, and 59.7\% lay in bursts. Under a \(\pm\)5.0~s burst window, only 17.0\% of swipes stood alone. Burst tendency differed widely between participants. The author set the burst window without a source.

\textbf{Limitations.} The present thesis has the following limits:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Muscle activity.} All three top features may partly reflect EMG (Section~\ref{sec:lit_emg}). TP10 lies at the lower edge of the scalp, where the share of EMG is largest, even at rest. AF7 and AF8 lie near the forehead muscle. Near a tensed muscle, EMG reaches every band, delta included. Common cleaning methods such as ICA reduce EMG without removing EMG completely.
    \item \textbf{Powerline-noise.} AF7 high-gamma mean (40--60~Hz) and TP10 raw standard deviation both contain the 50~Hz powerline-noise. No reported analysis used a notch filter (Section~\ref{sec:lit_notch_high_gamma}).
    \item \textbf{Suspected noise in the top features.} A rough check after the main analysis raised a suspicion that powerline-noise makes up a large part of the raw signal, and leaks into other bands, such as delta at AF8. The same check suggested that noise alone may produce Peak Frequency and Macro Frequency values similar to those of the recordings. The separation of some top features may therefore partly reflect noise. The suspicion awaits a proper test.
    \item \textbf{No artifact removal.} The artifact check flagged blinks and EMG, and removed nothing (Section~\ref{sec:method_artifact_analysis}).
    \item \textbf{Participants and headbands.} Two headband units were used. Differences between the two units cannot be separated from differences between participants. TP10 raw standard deviation is not z-scored, so TP10 raw standard deviation may partly reflect which participant and which headband was recorded (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}).
    \item \textbf{Sample.} The sample was demographically narrow and academically oriented, and each participant was recorded once. Three analysed participants reported an ADHD or attention-related diagnosis, and one reported taking psychoactive medication. ADHD and psychoactive medication were no exclusion criteria (Section~\ref{sec:method_participants}). About half of the analysed participants received a 20-euro voucher. The effect of the voucher is beyond the scope of the present thesis.
    \item \textbf{Keypresses.} Participants reported keypress errors for about 1\% of keypresses (Section~\ref{sec:method_participants}). Unreported keypress errors cannot be ruled out. The delay between each swipe and its ``A'' keypress was not measured.
    \item \textbf{\texttt{SKIP} windows in bursts.} In bursts, 23\% of the \texttt{SKIP} windows lie within the pre-swipe gap (2.0~s) or the post-swipe gap (0.5~s) of a neighbouring swipe. These \texttt{SKIP} windows may contain preparation or movement of the neighbouring swipe, and were kept (Section~\ref{sec:op_stay_skip}).
    \item \textbf{Statistics.} The \(p\)-values are not corrected for multiple comparisons, and serve as screening only (Section~\ref{sec:method_metrics_and_significance}).
    \item \textbf{Sensitivity analysis.} The sensitivity analysis was a rough check. The sensitivity analysis crossed the pre-swipe gap and the SKIP period without a statistical test, varied the window length alone at a fixed 1.0~s stride, and pooled all participants. Whether the effect of the window length depends on the pre-swipe gap or the SKIP period, or differs between participants, is untested (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}).
\end{itemize}
Part of what looks like a brain signal may be jaw or forehead muscle activity. Measuring how much comes from the brain and how much from muscles is the most important next step. The findings can be trusted only after the share of brain activity and the share of muscle activity are measured.

\textbf{Method.} The present thesis also leaves a working method. Naturalistic use of an algorithmic feed, with no video list chosen by the researcher, no fixation and no trial structure, was recorded on consumer hardware, with a keypress marking every swipe. The full pipeline ran from recording to results with open-source code, and used no data from TikTok (Section~\ref{sec:results_pipeline}). The full pipeline lowers the barrier to such studies, and remains a research tool (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}).

\textbf{Definition.} Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated} draws one further conclusion, beyond TikTok. Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated} proposes a definition of engagement that names no measuring instrument, four reporting requirements (object, interval, indicator, exit criterion), and a hypothesis for cheaper field measures where EEG cannot be used at scale. The hypothesis awaits testing.

Social media designers are tasked with maximising time on site \cite{baughan_i_2022}. In the literature reviewed here, long viewing can reflect attention held on the content or zoned-out viewing, normative dissociation (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_short_form}). The present thesis has not decoded the swipe. The present thesis offers a small first step toward measuring, independently of the platforms, the moment a viewer leaves.

\section{Future Research}
\label{sec:future_research}

\textbf{Replication.} Future research should first replicate the present findings in an independent sample. A replication would ideally use one headband unit, artifact control, a broader and less academically oriented sample, and correction for multiple comparisons. Clear exclusion criteria for ADHD and psychoactive medication, and a fixed briefing script (Section~\ref{sec:results_exp_learnings}), would reduce variation between participants and sessions. Webcam video, which the recording pipeline can already record (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_recording_pipeline}), could measure the delay between each swipe and its ``A'' keypress. A key test is whether the three top features, TP10 raw standard deviation, AF7 high-gamma mean and AF8 delta peak frequency, stay informative across sessions, content types and user groups. Whether the 20-euro voucher affected the data also remains to be tested.

\textbf{Brain or artifact.} The present results leave open whether the top features reflect brain activity, muscle activity, powerline-noise or the person recorded. Future work should control EMG in all bands, delta included, and not only in high gamma. Useful steps include motion monitoring, ICA, ICA with extra EMG recordings as in Li et al.\ \cite{li_electromyogram_2021}, channel-quality checks and checks per headband unit. A test without a person attached, as in Pattisapu and Ray \cite{pattisapu_stimulus-induced_2023}, could show how much powerline-noise the Muse~S picks up, and whether the Muse~S shows extra peaks at 14, 36 and 64~Hz. Comparing results before and after conservative cleaning would show how much of the separation remains. Recording handedness and the hand holding the phone would test the difference between left and right electrodes (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}).
